message.content:
{
    "short_answer": "Neurofibromatosis type 1",
    "long_answer": "Lisch nodules (iris hamartomas) together with multiple café-au-lait macules are classic diagnostic hallmarks of Neurofibromatosis type 1 (von Recklinghausen disease). Even in the absence of other manifestations such as cutaneous neurofibromas or optic glioma, these two findings fulfill the NIH criteria for NF1, an autosomal-dominant disorder caused by pathogenic variants in the NF1 gene on chromosome 17."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Alphabetical compilations of diseases and disorders—such as the widely cited Wikipedia “List of diseases (H)” and similar medical reference lists—include Histrionic Personality Disorder under the heading for the letter “H.” The condition is recognized in psychiatric nosology (e.g., DSM-5) and is routinely catalogued with other disorders that begin with the letter H. Therefore, it is correct to say that histrionic personality disorder is listed among diseases/disorders starting with “H.”"
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gallaudet, Usher syndrome",
    "long_answer": "The 2006 campus-wide protest occurred at Gallaudet University after the selection of Jane Fernandes—born deaf but an adult learner of American Sign Language—as president. The most frequent genetic cause of combined deafness and blindness is Usher syndrome, an autosomal-recessive disorder characterized by congenital sensorineural hearing loss and progressive retinitis pigmentosa."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Musculocutaneous nerve",
    "long_answer": "In Emery–Dreifuss muscular dystrophy the earliest clinical weakness appears in the humeral (upper-arm) muscles, especially the biceps brachii and brachialis. These muscles are innervated by the musculocutaneous nerve, so this nerve supplies the muscle group first affected in the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "National Organization for Rare Disorders",
    "long_answer": "The National Organization for Rare Disorders (NORD) is the primary patient-advocacy group that offers information, support resources, and connection to services for individuals and families living with Sabinas brittle hair syndrome and other rare genetic conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Follow-up",
    "long_answer": "In clinical documentation, the follow-up section records a patient’s subsequent progress, outcomes and any new findings after treatment or discharge. Regular follow-up is essential for conditions such as Asperger syndrome to monitor developmental changes, adjust interventions and evaluate long-term results."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bone morphogenetic protein (BMP)",
    "long_answer": "During limb development, secreted bone morphogenetic proteins—especially BMP-4—activate the apoptotic pathway in the interdigital mesenchyme. This programmed cell death removes the webbing that initially joins the digits, thereby preventing syndactyly (fusion of fingers or toes)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "American Journal of Psychiatry",
    "long_answer": "The epidemiological survey that quantified the prevalence of schizoid personality disorder among homeless adults living in New York City shelters was reported in the American Journal of Psychiatry, the flagship clinical research journal of the American Psychiatric Association."
}

{
    "short_answer": "European Society for Medical Oncology (ESMO)",
    "long_answer": "In the 2016 ESMO Clinical Practice Guidelines for metastatic colorectal cancer, the European Society for Medical Oncology explicitly stated that routine pre-treatment genotyping for the DPYD*2A variant (a marker of dihydropyrimidine dehydrogenase deficiency) is not recommended prior to administering 5-fluorouracil; testing should be reserved for cases with unexpected severe toxicity. Thus, the 2016 ESMO guideline did not advocate upfront DPYD*2A screening in metastatic CRC patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Medical diagnosis",
    "long_answer": "The systematic clinical process of evaluating a patient’s signs, symptoms, examinations and test results to identify the underlying disease is called medical diagnosis. This diagnostic process determines that the patient’s manifestations are due to Brachydactyly-preaxial hallux varus syndrome, thereby explaining the observed symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "National Academies Press",
    "long_answer": "The National Academies Press, the publishing arm of the U.S. National Academies of Sciences, Engineering, and Medicine, makes its authoritative reports freely available online. Its open-access titles include monographs such as “Arsenic in Drinking Water,” “Toxicological Effects of Methylmercury,” and “Measuring Lead Exposure in Infants and Children,” each detailing the health hazards and biological mechanisms of toxicity for arsenic, mercury, and lead. Thus, this press is the source of open-access works on heavy-metal toxicity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prescriptivism",
    "long_answer": "In linguistics, prescriptivism (language prescription) is the practice of formulating and enforcing rules that specify which words or constructions are considered correct or preferred in public or professional usage. The choice to distinguish and prefer the form 'tachycardia' over the broader 'tachyarrhythmia' illustrates such prescriptive rule-setting, where authorities standardize terminology for clarity and consistency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fell syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Fell syndrome (also called Fell-McDowell syndrome) is a rare form of primary ciliary dyskinesia in which respiratory epithelial cells completely lack motile cilia (ciliary aplasia). Because the air-way mucosa is unable to clear mucus and pathogens, patients develop chronic sinusitis, recurrent bronchopulmonary infections and bronchiectasis. The defining histological feature is the total absence of cilia on the respiratory epithelium, fulfilling the question’s description."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Monarch Initiative",
    "long_answer": "The Monarch Initiative develops and maintains the Mondo Disease Ontology, which integrates OMIM, Orphanet, ICD, and other sources into a single, harmonized disease resource. This consolidated ontology explicitly lists Toriello–Carey syndrome as one of its disease entries, providing standardized identifiers and cross-references across databases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "KMT2D",
    "long_answer": "Dr. Margaret Adam’s Kabuki syndrome research at Seattle Children’s Hospital focuses on the KMT2D gene (formerly called MLL2). Pathogenic loss-of-function variants in KMT2D are the most common cause of Kabuki syndrome, and Dr. Adam studies how these mutations alter the gene’s histone-methyltransferase activity and lead to the characteristic developmental features of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hobart Cavanaugh",
    "long_answer": "Among the cast of the 1947 film “Driftwood” —which centers on a Rocky Mountain spotted fever outbreak—the actor whose birthplace is Virginia City, Nevada, is Hobart Cavanaugh (born September 22, 1886 in Virginia City, NV)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chronic renal failure",
    "long_answer": "Women with end-stage or chronic renal failure who require hemodialysis have altered folate metabolism and uremic metabolic disturbances; pregnancies in these dialysis-dependent mothers show a higher incidence of neural tube defects in the fetus compared with the general population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bladder calculi",
    "long_answer": "In bladder exstrophy, chronic mucosal exposure, urinary stasis, and recurrent infections predispose patients to precipitation of urinary salts. Over time this leads to formation of calculi within the urinary bladder—bladder calculi—which is recognized as a significant long-term complication."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SURF1",
    "long_answer": "Leigh syndrome caused by pathogenic mutations in the SURF1 gene—needed for proper assembly of cytochrome-c oxidase (complex IV)—is particularly vulnerable to additional complex IV inhibition. Carbon monoxide binds and blocks cytochrome-c oxidase; CO poisoning therefore further impairs the already deficient complex IV activity in SURF1-related Leigh syndrome, leading to abrupt clinical deterioration."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Microtubule proteins",
    "long_answer": "Michelin tire baby syndrome (familial multiple congenital circumferential skin creases) is caused by mutations in genes that encode microtubule-associated/tubulin proteins (e.g., MAPRE2, TUBB). Defective microtubule function impairs normal chondrocyte proliferation and skeletal growth, leading to the proportionate dwarfism observed in affected infants."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Corticosteroids",
    "long_answer": "Baritosis is a benign pneumoconiosis from inhaled barium dust; when symptoms such as cough or airway irritation arise, physicians may prescribe corticosteroids because these drugs suppress the pulmonary inflammatory response, lessening mucosal swelling and irritation in the airways."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Segmental infantile hemangioma",
    "long_answer": "PELVIS syndrome is part of the group of segmental infantile hemangioma syndromes: large, plaque-like hemangiomas present at birth or shortly thereafter that follow a developmental skin segment and are associated with visceral, genitourinary and spinal malformations. Thus, the cutaneous lesion defining PELVIS syndrome is a segmental infantile hemangioma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Poor; usually fatal",
    "long_answer": "Santos–Mateus–Leal syndrome has been reported only in a few infants, all of whom presented with multiple severe congenital malformations and died shortly after birth. No long-term survivors have been documented, so the prognosis is considered uniformly poor and typically lethal in the neonatal period."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Disruption",
    "long_answer": "Graham-Boyle-Troxell syndrome is another name for the amniotic-band disruption complex. In this condition the embryo starts developing normally, but rupture of the amnion produces fibrous bands that mechanically destroy or constrict previously formed tissues, leading to limb defects, body-wall clefts and other injuries. Because the abnormalities result from the breakdown (disruption) of structures that had developed correctly, it is classified as a congenital disruption, not a primary malformation or deformation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Schwannomatosis",
    "long_answer": "So-called Neurofibromatosis type 4 is now recognized as the schwannomatosis form of neurofibromatosis.  It is a tumour-predisposition condition in which affected individuals develop multiple benign schwannomas (peripheral nerve-sheath tumours) but lack the classic vestibular (acoustic) schwannomas of NF2.  Thus, the condition associated with “Neurofibromatosis type 4” is schwannomatosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Keratin",
    "long_answer": "Regardless of the nail malformations seen in Cooks syndrome, the main structural substance of human nails remains keratin, a hard, sulfur-rich filament-forming protein that provides the nail plate with its characteristic strength and rigidity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cryptorchidism",
    "long_answer": "Cryptorchidism is the congenital condition in which one or both testes fail to descend into the scrotum. It is frequently reported among the urogenital anomalies that can accompany arthrogryposis multiplex congenita."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypertension",
    "long_answer": "Because the single kidney in unilateral renal agenesis must sustain the entire glomerular filtration load, it undergoes compensatory hyperfiltration and glomerular hypertrophy. Over years this can lead to glomerulosclerosis and impaired renal autoregulation, predisposing many adults with congenital solitary kidneys to develop systemic hypertension as a significant long-term complication."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Heat stroke",
    "long_answer": "Hamanishi Ueba Tsuji syndrome can involve hypohidrosis (markedly reduced sweating). Because sweating is the main mechanism for dissipating body heat, impaired sweat production hampers thermoregulation. This can allow core temperature to rise rapidly during warm environments or physical activity, predisposing affected individuals to serious overheating and potentially life-threatening heat stroke."
}

{
    "short_answer": "REV3L",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic heterozygous variants in the REV3L gene, which maps to chromosome 6q21, have been reported in patients showing the characteristic chest-wall and upper-limb defects of Poland syndrome (often in combination with Möbius sequence). This places the DNA polymerase-ζ catalytic subunit gene REV3L as the chromosome 6 locus most clearly implicated in Poland syndrome pathogenesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Joint arthroplasty",
    "long_answer": "In progressive pseudorheumatoid dysplasia, progressive cartilage loss leads to disabling pain and fixed joint contractures. When conservative measures (physiotherapy, NSAIDs) are insufficient, surgical joint arthroplasty—replacement of the affected joint with a prosthesis—can simultaneously relieve severe pain and correct the mechanical deformity responsible for contractures, restoring mobility and function."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PAX6 gene mutation",
    "long_answer": "Coloboma of the optic nerve and morning glory disc anomaly are both congenital optic-nerve malformations that have been repeatedly associated with heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the PAX6 gene. PAX6 encodes a paired-box homeodomain transcription factor critical for ocular morphogenesis; pathogenic variants disrupt optic-fissure closure and optic-disc development, giving rise to the spectrum that includes optic-nerve coloboma and morning glory disc anomaly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mesentery",
    "long_answer": "In patients with familial adenomatous polyposis, desmoid tumors arise predominantly in the intra-abdominal region, most often from the mesenteric connective tissue; this mesenteric location accounts for the majority of FAP-associated desmoids."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RMIT University",
    "long_answer": "Tony Briffa – an Australian intersex advocate born with partial androgen insensitivity syndrome – completed tertiary studies in electronics/avionics at RMIT University in Melbourne, Australia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HLA-DQB1",
    "long_answer": "The human leukocyte antigen class II gene HLA-DQB1, particularly the HLA-DQB1*06:02 allele, shows the strongest genetic association with narcolepsy. Variants within this locus are catalogued in dbSNP (e.g., rs3135388), confirming its presence in the database and its well-established link to the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Interstitial fluid reservoir",
    "long_answer": "In lymphatic malformations the malformed or absent lymphatic vessels are unable to clear the fluid that continually filters from blood capillaries. That fluid accumulates in the interstitium, which therefore serves as a low-pressure reservoir where lymph pools and progressively dilates sequestered lymphatic spaces, producing the characteristic cystic swelling of the lesion."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Jaw jerk reflex",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary spastic paraplegia involves degeneration of corticospinal and often corticobulbar upper-motor-neuron pathways. Loss of normal corticobulbar inhibition makes brain-stem stretch reflexes hyperactive; therefore the trigeminal-mediated jaw jerk reflex is frequently markedly brisk or ‘pronounced’ in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CREBBP",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the CREBBP gene (CREB-binding protein), mapped to chromosome 16p13.3, cause Rubinstein–Taybi syndrome. This syndrome is well known for its distinctive craniofacial appearance—down-slanting palpebral fissures, beaked nose, and broad thumbs—making CREBBP the chromosome-16 gene mutation classically linked to a characteristic facial gestalt."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Large-volume paracentesis",
    "long_answer": "Removing several litres of ascitic fluid without adequate plasma-volume expansion (e.g., albumin) can acutely decrease effective circulating volume, intensify splanchnic vasodilation, lower renal perfusion pressure and precipitate the functional renal failure characteristic of hepatorenal syndrome in cirrhotic patients. This procedure-related hypovolaemia is therefore a well-recognised iatrogenic trigger of HRS."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Greek paronychia",
    "long_answer": "The term for inflammation around the nail, “paronychia,” comes from the Ancient Greek word παρωνυχία (paronychía): para- (“beside, around”) + onyx/onych- (“nail”)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ophthalmology",
    "long_answer": "Ornithinaemia (hyper-ornithinaemia) is classically caused by deficiency of ornithine-δ-aminotransferase, producing gyrate atrophy of the choroid and retina with progressive night-blindness and visual field loss. Because the cardinal manifestations are ocular and management focuses on preserving vision, patients are most commonly treated by ophthalmologists."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mouse bioassay",
    "long_answer": "Researchers verified that scrapie prions remained in soil for prolonged periods by intracerebrally inoculating susceptible laboratory mice with soil extracts and monitoring them for prion disease; the development of scrapie in these mice (a mouse bioassay) confirmed the long-term persistence and infectivity of the prions in the soil."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Venglustat",
    "long_answer": "Venglustat (GZ/SAR402671, also called ibiglustat) is an orally bio-available, brain-penetrant inhibitor of glucosylceramide synthase. It is in clinical trials as a substrate-reduction therapy for GM2 gangliosidoses (Tay-Sachs and Sandhoff disease). The same mechanism—lowering glucosylceramide levels, which can drive α-synuclein aggregation—underpins separate trials of venglustat in synucleinopathies, including dementia with Lewy bodies. Thus venglustat is the investigational agent common to both GM2 gangliosidoses and Lewy body dementia studies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Tetrasomy X (48,XXXX) is a chromosomal aneuploidy that is diagnosed by cytogenetic testing—most commonly a standard karyotype or chromosomal microarray performed on peripheral blood lymphocytes or prenatal samples obtained by amniocentesis or chorionic-villus sampling. These tests require only a blood draw or routine prenatal fluid sampling, not a tissue biopsy. Therefore, a biopsy is not required to establish the diagnosis of tetrasomy X."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Infliximab",
    "long_answer": "Infliximab is a chimeric IgG1 monoclonal antibody that binds with high affinity to both soluble and trans-membrane tumor necrosis factor-α (TNF-α), thereby neutralizing its pro-inflammatory activity. It has been used off-label in refractory adult-onset Still's disease to control systemic inflammation and arthritis by blocking TNF-α–mediated signaling."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Swordfish",
    "long_answer": "Swordfish (Xiphias gladius) have an elongated, torpedo-shaped body and a pronounced, spear-like upper jaw. As long-lived, high-trophic-level predators, they bioaccumulate methylmercury to concentrations that often exceed safe dietary limits, which is why health agencies advise limiting their consumption."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cytochrome P450 2A6",
    "long_answer": "Valproate undergoes minor oxidative biotransformation to hepatotoxic metabolites such as 4-ene-valproic acid. This step is catalysed by the liver microsomal enzyme cytochrome P450 2A6 (CYP2A6). The CYP2A6 gene is located in the cytochrome P450 cluster on chromosome 19q13.2, fulfilling both criteria of being involved in valproate metabolism and residing on chromosome 19q."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bacillus Calmette-Guérin",
    "long_answer": "Intravesical instillation of the live attenuated Bacillus Calmette-Guérin (BCG) vaccine is the standard adjuvant therapy for non-muscle-invasive (superficial) transitional cell carcinoma of the bladder. The solution is introduced directly into the bladder, where BCG stimulates a local immune response that eliminates malignant urothelial cells and reduces recurrence and progression rates."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fragile X syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Fragile X syndrome, caused by CGG repeat expansion in the FMR1 gene on the X chromosome, is strongly linked with neurodevelopmental problems: 60–80 % of affected males and a substantial proportion of females meet criteria for attention deficit hyperactivity disorder. Large epidemiologic and clinical studies consistently show an incidence of ADHD several-fold higher in children with Fragile X than in the general population, making this genetic disorder a well-established risk factor for ADHD."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Congenital malformations",
    "long_answer": "In industrialized Western countries, structural or functional birth defects—grouped epidemiologically as congenital malformations—account for the largest proportion of deaths occurring before the first birthday. These lethal anomalies produce a characteristic deficit in the very base of the population pyramid (the 0-1-year age bar), making them readily visible when age-specific mortality is graphed for the population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Corrective eyeglasses",
    "long_answer": "In people with GAPO syndrome, the myopia (nearsightedness) that can accompany the disorder is managed in the same way as ordinary myopia—by prescribing corrective, concave eyeglass lenses that refocus incoming light onto the retina and restore clear distance vision."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vitamin D",
    "long_answer": "Wieacker (Wieacker-Wolff) syndrome is a neuromuscular disorder characterized by congenital contractures and progressive muscle weakness. Adequate vitamin D is essential for normal muscle function and bone integrity; deficiency causes myopathic weakness, hypotonia and decreased bone strength. Because patients with Wieacker syndrome already have marked muscle hypotonia and joint contractures, an accompanying vitamin D deficiency can aggravate their muscle weakness and skeletal problems, thereby worsening overall clinical symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Failure to thrive",
    "long_answer": "If alpha-thalassemia–related anemia is not recognized and treated, the persistent lack of hemoglobin limits oxygen delivery to tissues. In infants this chronic hypoxia can lead to poor weight gain, inadequate linear growth, and delayed physical development, collectively described clinically as “failure to thrive.”"
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dutasteride",
    "long_answer": "A two-year randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial tested the 5-α-reductase inhibitor dutasteride in men with Spinal and Bulbar Muscular Atrophy (Kennedy’s disease). After 24 months of treatment, quantitative muscle assessment and other functional measures showed no statistically significant difference between the dutasteride and placebo groups, indicating that the drug did not improve muscle strength in SBMA."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Foundational Model of Anatomy (FMA)",
    "long_answer": "The Foundational Model of Anatomy ontology is specifically designed to represent and classify human anatomical structures. It contains a detailed hierarchy in which the parotid gland is defined as an organ, subclassified under salivary glands, and linked to its anatomical relations (e.g., parts, locations, and neighboring structures). Thus, the anatomical classification of the parotid gland resides in the FMA ontology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dustin Hoffman",
    "long_answer": "Dustin Hoffman had a guest-star appearance on the television crime-drama series “Naked City” (episode aired 1962).  He later head-lined the 1995 motion picture “Outbreak,” whose plot is built around the Ebola virus disease.  Ebola virus infection is a hemorrhagic fever that, among its systemic manifestations, commonly produces ocular irritation with marked photophobia (light sensitivity) due to conjunctival inflammation.  Thus Hoffman links the rare-disease film centred on a photosensitivity-associated illness and an acting credit in Naked City."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Helicobacter pylori",
    "long_answer": "A frequent complication of polycythemia vera is peptic ulcer disease, which is strongly linked to infection with the bacterium Helicobacter pylori; thus, H. pylori is the organism associated with this common complication in PV patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ophthalmoscope",
    "long_answer": "Retinal capillary hemangioblastomas (retinal angiomas) in Von Hippel–Lindau disease are detected by ophthalmoscopy; the ophthalmoscope allows direct visualization of the fundus so that these vascular tumors can be identified and monitored."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SUCLA2 deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the SUCLA2 gene produce mitochondrial DNA depletion syndrome type 5. This disorder leads to encephalomyopathy with secondary mtDNA depletion in muscle and brain, and is well-documented to manifest hyperkinetic movement disorders such as dystonia and chorea because of basal ganglia involvement."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Incision and curettage",
    "long_answer": "Chalazia that remain large or persistent after conservative measures (warm compresses, topical antibiotics, or steroid injection) are surgically managed by incision and curettage, in which a small incision is made in the eyelid to allow complete curettage (scraping) and removal of the retained meibomian gland contents."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Facial nerve (CN VII)",
    "long_answer": "In facioscapulohumeral muscular dystrophy, weakness or paralysis of the facial muscles reflects dysfunction of the muscles supplied by the facial nerve (cranial nerve VII), the sole motor nerve that innervates the muscles of facial expression."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tuberous sclerosis",
    "long_answer": "The autosomal-dominant disorder tuberous sclerosis (mutations in TSC1 or TSC2) predisposes patients to develop subependymal giant-cell astrocytomas along the walls of the lateral ventricles, typically at the foramen of Monro. When these tumors enlarge, they can obstruct CSF flow from the lateral ventricles into the third ventricle, effectively blocking the third-ventricular outlet and producing obstructive hydrocephalus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Splenomegaly",
    "long_answer": "Type III galactosemia (UDP-galactose-4-epimerase deficiency) often leads to accumulation of toxic galactose metabolites that injure the liver and reticuloendothelial system. This results in enlargement of both liver and spleen; thus, splenomegaly—an abnormally enlarged spleen—is a characteristic clinical finding in many affected patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Stratified squamous epithelium",
    "long_answer": "Sheila Chandra retired because she developed burning mouth syndrome, a chronic neuropathic pain condition that affects the mucosal lining of the oral cavity (tongue, lips, palate, buccal mucosa). These oral surfaces are covered by stratified squamous epithelium, mostly non-keratinised, so this epithelial type is the one involved in the disorder that ended her singing career."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fluorescence in situ hybridization",
    "long_answer": "The chromosomal translocation t(7;17)(p15;q21), which creates the JAZF1-SUZ12 fusion characteristic of endometrial stromal sarcoma, can be directly visualized under a fluorescence microscope using fluorescence in situ hybridization (FISH). Labeled DNA probes that flank the breakpoint on chromosomes 7 and 17 hybridize to interphase nuclei or metaphase spreads, and the resulting split or fused fluorescent signals reveal the specific translocation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Paradoxical air embolism",
    "long_answer": "HHT patients often have pulmonary arteriovenous malformations that create right-to-left shunts, bypassing the lung’s filter. During intravenous therapy, even tiny air bubbles or particulates can pass through these shunts into the arterial circulation, leading to paradoxical air emboli that may cause stroke or brain abscess. Thus, air embolism is a recognized complication requiring filtered IV lines in HHT."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Metoclopramide",
    "long_answer": "Metoclopramide is a dopamine-2 receptor antagonist and 5-HT4 agonist that accelerates gastric and small-bowel transit while exerting central anti-emetic effects. Because patients with chronic intestinal pseudo-obstruction often suffer from nausea due to impaired motility, metoclopramide is routinely used as a prokinetic anti-emetic to reduce their nausea and improve gastrointestinal transit."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Uterus",
    "long_answer": "During systemic anaphylaxis, mast-cell mediators such as histamine, leukotrienes and prostaglandins trigger intense smooth-muscle contraction throughout the body. In women this can involve the smooth muscle of the uterus, leading to acute uterine contractions that are perceived as severe crampy lower-abdominal pain. Thus, the uterus is the female reproductive organ that can manifest painful cramps as a recognized symptom of anaphylaxis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RDX",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the RDX gene, which encodes the cytoskeletal protein radixin, are known to produce autosomal-recessive nonsyndromic deafness (DFNB24). The official gene symbol \"RDX\" is exactly the same acronym used for the high-explosive compound Research Department Explosive (cyclotrimethylenetrinitramine), making it the deafness gene whose name is shared with a widely used military explosive."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Migraine with aura",
    "long_answer": "Migraine with aura, especially the brainstem (basilar) subtype, is a neurological disorder whose aura phase can include pronounced truncal ataxia and vertigo that render the patient temporarily unable to stand or walk. Epidemiological studies consistently show that migraine with aura is an independent risk factor for ischaemic stroke, approximately doubling stroke risk, particularly in young and middle-aged adults."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Supportive care",
    "long_answer": "Before specific antivirals such as cidofovir or tecovirimat were developed, smallpox patients received only supportive care—measures like hydration, analgesics, antipyretics, and treatment of secondary bacterial infections—to relieve fever, pain, and dehydration because no drug directly inhibited the variola virus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SUMF1",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the SUMF1 gene, which encodes the formyl-glycine-generating enzyme essential for activating all sulfatases, are responsible for multiple sulfatase deficiency. The same pathogenic SUMF1 variants have also been documented in individuals who manifest linear porokeratosis, establishing SUMF1 mutation as the common genetic link between the two conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Turricephaly",
    "long_answer": "Turricephaly is a form of craniosynostosis in which premature fusion of the coronal and often lambdoid sutures restricts lateral and anteroposterior skull growth, forcing compensatory vertical expansion that produces a very tall, tower-shaped head. Because the scalp must drape over this abnormally elevated cranial vault, redundant skin folds can develop, and these corrugated folds are recognized clinically as cutis verticis gyrata, making CVG an occasional associated finding in turricephaly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TDP1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the TDP1 gene (tyrosyl-DNA phosphodiesterase 1) cause spinocerebellar ataxia with axonal neuropathy (SCAN1). TDP1 is a DNA repair enzyme that removes covalently trapped topoisomerase I from DNA ends, a critical step in the single-strand break repair pathway. Thus, this spinocerebellar ataxia-associated gene is directly involved in DNA repair processes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TGA can herald MI",
    "long_answer": "Although rare, acute myocardial infarction has been documented as a precipitating event for transient global amnesia; the sudden cardiac ischemia and catecholamine surge can cause brief hippocampal dysfunction, so an episode of TGA may be the first clinical sign of an underlying myocardial infarction."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Zidovudine",
    "long_answer": "Zidovudine (AZT) is a nucleoside reverse-transcriptase inhibitor used in combination antiretroviral therapy for HIV infection. According to its FDA-approved prescribing information and multiple clinical studies, common adverse reactions include headache, anemia, gastrointestinal upset, and asthenia (generalized weakness). Thus, zidovudine is a well-documented HIV medication in which asthenia is a recognized side effect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurogenic bowel",
    "long_answer": "During Phase 4 of spinal shock the spinal reflex arcs below the lesion re-appear without descending (supraspinal) control.  This permanently disrupts coordinated voluntary defecation, producing a spastic, hyper-reflexic bowel with impaired emptying and episodes of fecal incontinence or constipation—clinically termed a neurogenic bowel."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Norepinephrine",
    "long_answer": "In Catecholaminergic Polymorphic Ventricular Tachycardia (CPVT), adrenergic stress provokes ventricular tachyarrhythmias. The key mediator is the catecholaminergic neurotransmitter norepinephrine, released from sympathetic nerves during exercise or emotional stress. Norepinephrine binds β-adrenergic receptors on cardiomyocytes, raising intracellular cAMP, enhancing Ca²⁺ influx and ryanodine-receptor–mediated sarcoplasmic Ca²⁺ release. This Ca²⁺ overload triggers delayed after-depolarizations that precipitate the polymorphic ventricular tachycardia characteristic of CPVT."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Central core disease (CCD) is a congenital myopathy most commonly caused by pathogenic variants in the ryanodine-receptor 1 (RYR1) gene. Histologically, affected muscle fibres show one or more central \"cores\" that are depleted of mitochondria and oxidative enzymes and display disorganisation or loss of sarcomeric (myofibrillar) structure. The disease is not characterised by active regeneration or increased synthesis of contractile proteins; instead there is relative hypotrophy of type-1 fibres, lack of necrosis-regeneration cycles, and, within the cores, a reduction—not an increase—of myofibrils. Hence, myofibril production is not up-regulated in central core disease; rather, myofibrillar content is diminished or structurally abnormal in the core regions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 17",
    "long_answer": "About 60–70 % of osteogenesis imperfecta cases arise from mutations in the COL1A1 gene, which encodes the α-1 chain of type I collagen and is located on chromosome 17 (17q21.31-q22). Defects in COL1A1 impair type I collagen synthesis or structure, leading to the bone fragility characteristic of osteogenesis imperfecta. While COL1A2 on chromosome 7 also contributes, chromosome 17 mutations account for the majority of cases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FRS2",
    "long_answer": "Low-grade osteosarcomas often harbor amplification of the FRS2 gene (Fibroblast Growth Factor Receptor Substrate 2). The FRS2 adaptor protein binds phosphorylated receptor tyrosine kinases (e.g., FGFR, EGFR, PDGFR) and recruits GRB2/SOS, SHP2 and PI3K complexes, thereby transmitting signals to the RAS-MAPK, PI3K-AKT and other downstream pathways. This function of linking activated RTKs to multiple signaling cascades identifies FRS2 as the relevant gene."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cholestyramine",
    "long_answer": "Cholestyramine is a bile-acid–binding resin. In cholestasis, it relieves pruritus by sequestering bile acids in the intestine, decreasing their systemic accumulation. The same bile-acid sequestration lowers circulating LDL-cholesterol, so cholestyramine is also prescribed to reduce hyperlipidemia and thereby treat or prevent coronary artery disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Frameshift mutation",
    "long_answer": "Fryns-Aftimos syndrome is produced by a frameshift (loss-of-function) mutation that shifts the reading frame, creates a premature stop codon, and eliminates production of the normal protein. The resulting lack of functional protein is responsible for the multiple congenital anomalies that characterize the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Diaphragm",
    "long_answer": "The diaphragm is the principal muscle of respiration in humans. Its rhythmic contraction increases thoracic volume and lowers intrathoracic pressure, drawing air into the lungs; relaxation allows passive exhalation. Proper diaphragmatic function is therefore essential for ventilation and for managing conditions such as pulmonary heart disease, where effective breathing reduces right-heart strain and improves oxygenation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Thymus",
    "long_answer": "In many infants with congenital rubella syndrome heart surgery is required, and surgeons often excise the thymus to gain access to the mediastinum. The thymus is the primary site where T lymphocytes mature and become functional. Neonatal or early-infancy thymectomy therefore markedly impairs T-cell–mediated immunity, leaving the child more susceptible to bacterial, viral and fungal infections. Hence, removal of the thymus increases the risk of infection in these infants."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fetal alcohol syndrome",
    "long_answer": "When a pregnant woman drinks, ethanol readily crosses the placenta and accumulates in the embryo-fetus. Alcohol and its metabolite acetaldehyde act as teratogens: they impair cell proliferation, disrupt neural crest migration, induce oxidative stress, and alter gene expression during critical periods of organogenesis. These mechanisms produce the characteristic constellation of growth restriction, craniofacial abnormalities, and central nervous system defects collectively known as fetal alcohol syndrome, a major cause of birth defects."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pediatric hematology-oncology",
    "long_answer": "Kasabach–Merritt syndrome is a life-threatening consumptive coagulopathy caused by vascular tumors such as kaposiform hemangioendothelioma. Management requires correction of severe thrombocytopenia and coagulopathy and initiation of systemic antitumor therapies (e.g., corticosteroids, vincristine, sirolimus), all of which fall within the expertise of pediatric hematologists/oncologists. Therefore, infants with this syndrome are most commonly treated under a pediatric hematology-oncology service."
}

{
    "short_answer": "The face",
    "long_answer": "Pilotto syndrome primarily causes congenital malformations of the facial region. The face is the bodily area most critical for personal recognition and for conveying emotions through facial expressions; therefore, facial involvement in Pilotto syndrome directly impacts these fundamental human functions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Flexor carpi ulnaris",
    "long_answer": "In amyoplasia-type arthrogryposis, the wrist is typically fixed in flexion and ulnar deviation. Surgical correction frequently combines a dorsal carpal wedge osteotomy with a tendon transfer that recruits the flexor carpi ulnaris muscle, rerouting it to wrist extensors such as extensor carpi radialis brevis, to restore active wrist extension and improve hand positioning."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Exon 5",
    "long_answer": "Parastremmatic dwarfism is produced by pathogenic missense mutations located in exon 5 of the TRPV4 gene (e.g., the recurrent c.805C>T, p.Arg269Cys/His change). These exon-5 variants alter the ankyrin-repeat domain of the TRPV4 calcium-permeable channel, leading to the characteristic severe spondylometaphyseal dysplasia known as parastremmatic dwarfism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ventricular fibrillation",
    "long_answer": "The child’s cardiac arrest progressed to ventricular fibrillation, an immediately life-threatening arrhythmia in which the ventricles quiver chaotically instead of contracting effectively. This rhythm is regarded as the most severe form of cardiac arrest because it abolishes all effective cardiac output and demands prompt defibrillation to prevent death."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Medical genetics",
    "long_answer": "Sakati–Nyhan–Tisdale syndrome is a rare inherited skeletal-endocrine disorder; its recognition, genetic confirmation, counseling, and coordinated care fall within the domain of the medical genetics specialty, which manages congenital and hereditary conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TPM2",
    "long_answer": "Distal arthrogryposis (e.g., DA1 and Sheldon-Hall syndrome/DA2B) is most often linked to missense mutations in the beta-tropomyosin gene TPM2. This gene is located on chromosome 9p13.3, and pathogenic variants in TPM2 disrupt skeletal-muscle thin-filament function, producing the characteristic distal limb contractures."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Interleukin-10 (IL-10)",
    "long_answer": "IL-10 is a potent anti-inflammatory cytokine that restrains pathogenic Th1 and Th17 responses in the central nervous system. In mouse models, genetic deletion of IL-10 leads to earlier onset and markedly more severe clinical and histological manifestations of experimental autoimmune encephalomyelitis, demonstrating that IL-10 deficiency exacerbates EAE."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Distal ulna",
    "long_answer": "To correct the typical flexion-with-ulnar-deviation (“wind-blown”) wrist deformity seen in children with the amyoplasia form of arthrogryposis multiplex congenita, surgeons commonly perform a distal-ulna procedure—usually a distal-ulnar wedge/shortening osteotomy or partial distal-ulna resection—combined with tendon balancing. Working on the distal ulna allows the carpus and hand to be realigned into a more neutral, functional position."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Indometacin is extensively bound to serum albumin (≈90 %), so in vitro the free fraction of the drug rises when albumin concentrations fall.  However, for highly protein-bound drugs that have relatively low hepatic extraction and a short half-life—such as indometacin—an increase in the unbound fraction is usually offset by a parallel increase in clearance and distribution volume.  As a result, the steady-state unbound (pharmacologically active) concentration changes little and clinically important toxicity is not expected to increase solely because a patient has hypoalbuminaemia or otherwise low plasma-protein levels.  Standard drug references and regulatory product labeling for indometacin do not list low plasma protein states as a condition that heightens adverse-effect severity, in contrast to drugs with a narrow therapeutic index such as warfarin or phenytoin.  Therefore, current scientific and clinical consensus does not support the statement that indometacin causes more severe side effects in patients with low levels of plasma proteins."
}

{
    "short_answer": "United States",
    "long_answer": "McDonough syndrome is an extremely rare X-linked intellectual-developmental disorder that has only been described in a single American family; thus, all documented cases originate from the United States."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Perifollicular fibrosis",
    "long_answer": "In acquired pili torti, chronic inflammatory or scarring scalp disorders induce concentric fibrosis around the hair follicle (perifollicular fibrosis). This fibrotic contraction mechanically distorts and flattens the follicle, causing the emerging hair shaft to twist at irregular intervals—producing the characteristic tortuous hairs."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Randomized controlled trial",
    "long_answer": "The efficacy and development of virtual-reality exposure therapy for acrophobia have been established through randomized controlled trials, where height-phobic participants are randomly allocated to VR exposure, in-vivo exposure, or control conditions, permitting causal assessment of therapeutic benefit."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Psychotherapy",
    "long_answer": "Passive-aggressive (negativistic) personality disorder is primarily managed with structured psychotherapy—most commonly cognitive-behavioural or other insight-oriented talk therapies—which aim to help the patient recognise and modify maladaptive, indirectly hostile thought and behaviour patterns; no medication has proven curative, so psychotherapy is the standard therapeutic approach."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SLC20A2",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the phosphate transporter gene SLC20A2 are the most frequent genetic cause of primary familial brain calcification (PFBC). The same SLC20A2 mutations have been documented in families in which affected children initially presented with acute rheumatic-type chorea that met clinical criteria for Sydenham’s chorea. Thus, SLC20A2 mutations link both conditions, explaining brain calcifications as well as choreiform movements classically attributed to Sydenham’s chorea."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Amyl nitrite",
    "long_answer": "Amyl nitrite, popularly sold as “poppers,” is a volatile nitrite that people inhale for its rapid vasodilating and euphoric effects. Unlike most other inhalants (e.g., solvents, aerosols, nitrous oxide) that are not scheduled, amyl nitrite is specifically listed as a Schedule III substance under the U.S. Controlled Substances Act, making its manufacture, distribution, and possession federally regulated."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X-linked recessive loss-of-function",
    "long_answer": "Adrenoleukodystrophy results from loss-of-function mutations in the X-chromosomal ABCD1 gene, leading to deficient peroxisomal transport of very-long-chain fatty acids and their toxic accumulation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Goldenhar syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Goldenhar syndrome (oculo-auriculo-vertebral dysplasia) is a congenital genetic disorder characterized by the triad of craniofacial anomalies, auricular malformations, and vertebral segmentation defects. The vertebral defects often include hemivertebrae and congenital scoliosis. In addition, patients may have associated occult spinal dysraphism, such as lumbosacral dermoid or epidermoid cysts, tethered cord, or dermal sinuses. Thus, the combination of vertebral anomalies (e.g., scoliosis) with dermoid cysts in the lumbosacral region is a well-recognized manifestation of Goldenhar syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Thiazide diuretics",
    "long_answer": "In hyperkalemic periodic paralysis, thiazide-class diuretics (e.g., hydrochlorothiazide) are prescribed because they promote kaliuresis—enhancing renal excretion of potassium in the distal convoluted tubule. By lowering serum potassium, thiazide diuretics help prevent or abort paralytic attacks associated with this channelopathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RPL35",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the ribosomal protein gene RPL35 cause Diamond-Blackfan anemia, a form of congenital hypoplastic anemia. The RPL35 gene is mapped to chromosome 9q34, making it the chromosome-9 gene linked to this inherited red-cell aplasia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "POLG",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the POLG gene (encoding the catalytic subunit of mitochondrial DNA polymerase-γ) are a recognised cause of mitochondrial disorders that frequently present with chronic intestinal pseudo-obstruction. The same POLG mutations are well documented to produce early-onset or atypical Parkinson’s disease owing to mitochondrial dysfunction in dopaminergic neurons. Thus, POLG links both chronic intestinal pseudo-obstruction and Parkinson’s disease." 
}

{
    "short_answer": "SQSTM1",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the SQSTM1 gene, which encodes the autophagy-adaptor protein p62, are a common genetic cause of Paget disease of bone and are also repeatedly found in neurodegenerative conditions such as amyotrophic lateral sclerosis and frontotemporal dementia. Defective p62 disrupts ubiquitin-proteasome and autophagy pathways, producing abnormal bone remodeling alongside progressive neuronal loss, so the same SQSTM1 mutation often manifests with both skeletal disorders and neurodegeneration."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Previous abdominal surgery",
    "long_answer": "Idiopathic sclerosing mesenteritis has been repeatedly linked to a history of prior abdominal surgery; operative manipulation or trauma of the mesentery is thought to trigger the subsequent fibro-inflammatory reaction characteristic of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Parathion",
    "long_answer": "Parathion is a highly toxic organophosphate insecticide (O,O-diethyl O-p-nitrophenyl phosphorothioate). In chemical industry it is used as a starting material/intermediate for synthesising a few organophosphate-based pharmaceuticals (e.g., ophthalmic cholinesterase inhibitors). Inhalational exposure to parathion dust or vapour in production plants has been well documented to provoke IgE-mediated airway hyper-responsiveness and occupational asthma in exposed workers."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cardiorenal syndrome",
    "long_answer": "In VACTERL association, congenital heart malformations and renal anomalies can each compromise organ function. Impaired cardiac output reduces renal perfusion, while defective kidneys contribute to volume and pressure overload on the heart. This bidirectional worsening of heart and kidney performance is the pathophysiological basis of cardiorenal syndrome, making affected patients particularly susceptible to this condition."
}

{
        "short_answer": "Massachusetts",
        "long_answer": "Massachusetts fits both conditions. Demographic analyses from groups such as the UCLA Williams Institute show that roughly 0.6 % of the Commonwealth’s adult residents—tens of thousands of people—identify as transgender, placing it among the states with the largest transgender populations by percentage. In addition, Massachusetts became the first U.S. state to legalize same-sex marriage, when the Goodridge v. Department of Public Health decision took effect on 17 May 2004. Hence, Massachusetts is the state that satisfies both criteria."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pain at mealtimes",
    "long_answer": "Outward displacement of the earlobe points to parotid‐gland enlargement. When this enlargement is accompanied by sharp pain that intensifies during eating—when salivary flow is stimulated—it is typical of acute parotid sialadenitis, distinguishing it from usually painless parotid tumours."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Apiphobia",
    "long_answer": "The clinical term for an irrational, excessive fear of bees is apiphobia (also called melissophobia). As with other specific phobias, cultural narratives can amplify the fear; in this case, stories from African folklore and sensational reports of aggressive “Africanized” bees frequently reinforce and shape sufferers’ anxiety toward any type of bee."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HLA-B*15:02",
    "long_answer": "The HLA-B*15:02 allele is a well-established pharmacogenetic risk factor for life-threatening severe cutaneous adverse reactions (Stevens–Johnson syndrome/toxic epidermal necrolysis) triggered by carbamazepine. Because this variant occurs within the major histocompatibility complex, linkage with neighboring loci that influence biotin metabolism can result in reduced biotinidase activity, so carriers may also be predisposed to biotin deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Daentl-Townsend-Siegel syndrome is the original name given to the disorder now known as blepharophimosis-ptosis-epicanthus inversus syndrome (BPES). BPES is produced by heterozygous mutations in the FOXL2 gene on chromosome 3q23 and is characteristically transmitted from one affected parent to offspring in a vertical fashion with male-to-male transmission possible. This clear vertical transmission pattern establishes the syndrome’s mode of inheritance as autosomal dominant."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FRMD7",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the FRMD7 gene (mapped to Xq26) are the main known cause of X-linked idiopathic infantile nystagmus. Because FRMD7 lies on the X chromosome and its loss-of-function variants consistently produce congenital nystagmus, it is the prime candidate gene that could underlie the X-linked form historically referred to as Mietens syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Oxcarbazepine is an anticonvulsant structurally related to carbamazepine and is sometimes used off-label for neuropathic conditions such as notalgia paresthetica. A well-established part of its safety profile is cutaneous adverse reactions, ranging from mild morbilliform (maculopapular) rashes to severe drug eruptions such as Stevens-Johnson syndrome, toxic epidermal necrolysis and DRESS (Drug Reaction with Eosinophilia and Systemic Symptoms). These events are reported in 2–5 % of treated patients and are recognized in the prescribing information and multiple dermatology and pharmacovigilance reviews. Therefore, when a patient with notalgia paresthetica is treated with oxcarbazepine, the medication can indeed precipitate a drug eruption."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Primary cilia",
    "long_answer": "Primary (non-motile) cilia are microtubule-based organelles that mediate key developmental signalling pathways (e.g., Sonic Hedgehog). Mutations in genes encoding ciliary proteins disrupt these signals, impair midline fore-brain patterning, and commonly produce agenesis or hypogenesis of the corpus callosum, as seen in several ciliopathies (e.g., Joubert and acrocallosal syndromes)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sirolimus",
    "long_answer": "Sirolimus (rapamycin) is an mTOR-inhibiting immunosuppressant that is being used and studied to control seizures in tuberous-sclerosis-related epilepsy. Case reports have shown that systemic administration of sirolimus led to marked improvement and even remission of the cutaneous and gastrointestinal manifestations of Degos disease, making it a documented therapeutic option for both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Intestinal atresia",
    "long_answer": "In gastroschisis the bowel protrudes through a narrow abdominal wall defect, and the mesenteric vessels can be constricted while the exposed intestine is bathed in irritant amniotic fluid.  This combination often compromises blood flow, leading to ischemic injury of the small intestine.  The ischemia can progress to loss of a bowel segment, producing jejuno-ileal (intestinal) atresia, a dangerous complication that markedly increases morbidity in these infants."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Interleukin-6 (IL-6)",
    "long_answer": "Interleukin-6 is a pleiotropic cytokine whose placental production and maternal serum levels are markedly increased in pre-eclampsia, where it contributes to endothelial dysfunction, hypertension and the exaggerated inflammatory response characteristic of the disorder. Beyond pregnancy, IL-6 is a key immune regulator, driving acute-phase reactions, B-cell maturation and T-helper cell polarization, and it also acts directly on hematopoietic and other stem cells to promote their survival, proliferation and differentiation. This dual involvement in both pre-eclampsia pathogenesis and immune/stem-cell regulation identifies IL-6 as the relevant cytokine."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Methylation-sensitive Southern blot",
    "long_answer": "Facioscapulohumeral muscular dystrophy type 2 (FSHD2) is distinguished by marked hypomethylation of the D4Z4 repeat array on chromosome 4q35. Diagnostic laboratories assess this by digesting genomic DNA with methylation-sensitive restriction enzymes and then performing Southern blot hybridisation. The resulting methylation-sensitive Southern blot directly quantifies methylation levels at 4q35, allowing confirmation of FSHD2 when hypomethylation is detected."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Triple X syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Cytogenetic studies have shown that the sex-chromosome aneuploidy 47,XXX (Triple X syndrome) is detected several-fold more often in women diagnosed with schizophrenia than in the general female population (where it occurs in ≈1/1,000 births). The presence of an extra X chromosome is thought to disturb neurodevelopmental pathways, increasing vulnerability to psychotic disorders such as schizophrenia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Testosterone replacement therapy",
    "long_answer": "Leydig cell hypoplasia results from inactivating mutations in the LH/chorionic-gonadotropin receptor, so the testes cannot produce sufficient testosterone for masculinization. Administering exogenous testosterone (testosterone replacement therapy) bypasses the defective Leydig cells, providing the androgen needed to induce and sustain normal male pubertal and sexual development."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Erythromycin",
    "long_answer": "In Japan, long-term, low-dose erythromycin—originally introduced for diffuse panbronchiolitis—has been routinely and empirically prescribed for patients with primary ciliary dyskinesia to reduce airway inflammation and chronic bacterial infection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "N-acetylaspartate (NAA)",
    "long_answer": "Proton-MRS studies consistently show lower concentrations of the neuronal metabolite N-acetylaspartate (NAA) in the medial prefrontal cortex of patients with obsessive-compulsive disorder. NAA is considered a proxy of neuronal integrity and efficiency, and higher prefrontal NAA levels have been linked to better working-memory capacity and greater creative (divergent) thinking performance in healthy subjects. Thus, NAA is the molecule that is reduced in the mPFC in OCD and also serves as a marker of creativity and working memory." }

{
    "short_answer": "Medical genetics",
    "long_answer": "Short rib–polydactyly syndrome is a congenital, autosomal-recessive skeletal dysplasia. Diagnosis, counseling about inheritance risk, and coordination of multidisciplinary care are overseen by the medical genetics specialty."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Multi-hit hypothesis",
    "long_answer": "Chronic lymphocytic leukemia is best explained by the multi-hit (Knudson) hypothesis, which states that several successive genetic ‘hits’—such as 13q14 loss, trisomy 12, TP53 or ATM mutations—accumulate within a single B-cell clone. Each hit disables tumor-suppressor pathways or activates growth-promoting signals, progressively blocking apoptosis and conferring a survival advantage. The step-wise acquisition of these lesions drives the abnormal clonal expansion of mature B-lymphocytes that characterizes CLL."
}

{
    "short_answer": "AP4B1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the AP4B1 gene, which encodes the β-subunit of the adaptor protein complex-4, cause autosomal-recessive hereditary spastic paraplegia type 47 (SPG47). The AP4B1 locus is situated on chromosome 1p13.3, making it the HSP-associated gene located on chromosome 1."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ATM",
    "long_answer": "The ATM (ataxia-telangiectasia mutated) gene encodes a serine/threonine kinase that is rapidly activated by DNA double-strand breaks and orchestrates the DNA damage response by phosphorylating key substrates such as p53, BRCA1 and CHK2. Loss-of-function mutations in ATM cause the neurodegenerative disorder ataxia-telangiectasia, underscoring its central role in cellular repair mechanisms and neuronal survival."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bacillus cereus",
    "long_answer": "The spore-forming Gram-positive rod Bacillus cereus is an opportunistic pathogen that frequently causes bloodstream infections, meningitis and other severe illnesses in immunocompromised hosts. Acute leukemia patients, who are profoundly neutropenic because of their disease and chemotherapy, are particularly susceptible to B. cereus bacteremia and sepsis, making this bacterium a well-recognized cause of infections in leukemia patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 7",
    "long_answer": "Sabinas brittle hair syndrome is a non-photosensitive form of trichothiodystrophy caused by loss-of-function mutations in the TTDN1 (C7orf11) gene, which is located at 7q14.3 on human chromosome 7. Therefore, chromosome 7 is the chromosome associated with Sabinas brittle hair syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Electroencephalography (EEG)",
    "long_answer": "Benign familial infantile epilepsy is confirmed by performing an electroencephalogram (EEG), which detects the characteristic epileptiform discharges associated with this self-limited infantile seizure disorder and distinguishes it from other causes of seizures in infancy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Coronary artery disease",
    "long_answer": "In 46,XX gonadal dysgenesis the streak ovaries produce almost no estrogen. Estrogen normally raises HDL, lowers LDL, and protects vascular endothelium; its absence therefore accelerates atherosclerosis. Consequently, women with this condition have a markedly increased risk of premature coronary artery disease (ischemic heart disease), the principal cardiovascular complication linked to their hypo-estrogenic state."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PVC clothing",
    "long_answer": "In cardiology a “PVC” is a Premature Ventricular Contraction, an abnormal heart rhythm in which an ectopic ventricular beat occurs earlier than expected. The same three-letter term “PVC” is widely used in fashion to denote garments made of polyvinyl chloride—shiny, plastic-coated items such as PVC jackets, dresses or trousers. Thus, the garment type known as PVC clothing shares its name with this specific cardiac arrhythmia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Parvovirus B19 IgM antibody test",
    "long_answer": "Acute Parvovirus B19 infection that halts erythropoiesis and produces reticulocytopenia is confirmed serologically by detecting virus-specific IgM antibodies in the patient’s serum; the presence of Parvovirus B19 IgM indicates recent infection and establishes the diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bardet–Biedl syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Bardet–Biedl syndrome (BBS) is a pleiotropic ciliopathy in which the cardinal ocular manifestation is rod-cone dystrophy presenting as syndromic retinitis pigmentosa. Because the causal genes encode proteins of the primary cilium, multiple organ systems are affected; along with polydactyly, obesity, renal anomalies and hypogonadism, orofacial anomalies such as cleft lip and/or cleft palate are well-recognised secondary features. Thus BBS links syndromic retinitis pigmentosa with the possibility of cleft lip or palate in the same genetic disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Platelet transfusion",
    "long_answer": "In giant platelet disorders (e.g., Bernard-Soulier syndrome, MYH9-related disease) the platelets present are both reduced in number and functionally impaired, so the primary, evidence-based therapy for significant bleeding episodes or prior to surgery is transfusion of normal donor platelets. This temporarily supplies adequate numbers of functional platelets, correcting the hemostatic defect and controlling bleeding."
}

{
    "short_answer": "House, M.D.",
    "long_answer": "The medical drama “House, M.D.” features an episode in which a patient who recently went deer-hunting develops progressive weakness; Dr. House’s team discovers a tick hidden in the scalp and identifies tick paralysis. Removal of the tick resolves the neurotoxin-mediated, Guillain-Barré-like paralysis, a textbook presentation of the condition acquired after exposure in wooded hunting areas."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gynecology",
    "long_answer": "Persistent Genital Arousal Disorder is classified among female sexual‐function disorders. Women with PGAD are typically evaluated and treated by physicians trained in obstetrics-gynecology (often urogynecologists or sexual-medicine gynecologists), because this specialty manages female reproductive anatomy, hormonal factors, and pelvic-nerve or vascular issues implicated in the condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Brown adipose tissue (brown fat)",
    "long_answer": "Madelung's disease (multiple symmetric lipomatosis) is characterized by the uncontrolled proliferation and deposition of brown adipose tissue—also called brown fat—around the neck, shoulders, and upper trunk. Pathogenic studies show mitochondrial abnormalities within brown-fat adipocytes, linking this specific fat type to the disease process."
}

{
    "short_answer": "BRCA1 loss of heterozygosity",
    "long_answer": "If uniparental disomy duplicates the chromosome 17 segment that carries a pathogenic BRCA1 allele from one parent while deleting the normal allele from the other parent, it produces copy-neutral loss of heterozygosity. Both BRCA1 copies become mutated or absent, inactivating this key tumor-suppressor gene involved in homologous-recombination DNA repair. Biallelic BRCA1 inactivation removes a critical brake on genomic instability, thereby greatly increasing the risk of breast and ovarian cancer."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Keratinization",
    "long_answer": "In the ICD-O histologic definitions, squamous-cell carcinoma is identified by the production of keratin (often forming keratin pearls) and/or intercellular bridges within the tumor cells; this keratinization is the primary microscopic hallmark used to confirm the diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dorsal carpal wedge osteotomy",
    "long_answer": "In amyoplasia-type arthrogryposis the wrists are often fixed in severe flexion. When this contracture is excessive and resists splinting, the accepted operation is a dorsal carpal wedge osteotomy: a closing wedge of bone is removed from the dorsal aspect of the carpus, allowing the hand to be brought into a more functional, extended position and improving the child’s ability to reach and grasp. This procedure is widely recommended in the orthopedic literature for severe wrist flexion deformity in arthrogryposis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vitreous hemorrhage",
    "long_answer": "Optic disc drusen can occasionally rupture small peripapillary vessels, leading to bleeding that extends into the vitreous cavity. This spontaneous vitreous hemorrhage is an uncommon but documented complication of optic disc drusen, distinguished from more typical visual-field loss or retinal vascular events."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Noonan syndrome is a genetically heterogeneous RAS-pathway disorder characterized by distinctive facial features, short (but usually proportionate) stature, congenital heart defects (most commonly pulmonary valve stenosis), chest deformities, and variable developmental delay. Its accepted synonyms include "Noonan-Ehmke syndrome," "Turner-like syndrome," and "male Turner syndrome," among others.

The designation "mesomelic dwarfism-small genitalia syndrome" is not listed in the medical or genetic literature as a synonym for Noonan syndrome. Mesomelic dwarfism describes a class of skeletal dysplasias in which the middle segments of the limbs are disproportionately short; these conditions (e.g., Langer mesomelic dysplasia, Léri-Weill dyschondrosteosis) have different genetic causes, clinical features, and nomenclature from Noonan syndrome.

Therefore, saying that Noonan syndrome is also known as "mesomelic dwarfism-small genitalia syndrome" is not supported by established biomedical consensus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Predominant AT2-receptor expression",
    "long_answer": "In the fetus most angiotensin II binds to the angiotensin-II type-2 (AT2) receptor, which is highly expressed before birth. AT2-receptor signalling drives cellular growth, differentiation and tissue development, producing little vasoconstriction or sodium retention. After birth AT1 receptors become predominant, and angiotensin II then acts mainly as a pressor and volume-regulating hormone. This developmental switch in receptor subtype expression explains why the renin-angiotensin system operates differently in the fetus compared with the adult."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Transforming growth factor β1",
    "long_answer": "In biliary atresia, hepatic expression of transforming growth factor β1 (TGF-β1) is markedly up-regulated. TGF-β1 is a profibrogenic cytokine that activates hepatic stellate cells and stimulates extracellular matrix deposition, driving the progressive periportal and lobular fibrosis characteristic of the disease. Elevated TGF-β1 levels in liver tissue and serum of affected infants correlate with the severity of fibrosis, establishing its central role in the pathogenesis of biliary atresia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Anxiety",
    "long_answer": "Pyrophobia is classified as a specific phobia of fire. Like all specific phobias, its hallmark symptom is a surge of intense anxiety or panic when the person anticipates or encounters the feared stimulus. This anxiety can manifest with autonomic signs such as rapid heartbeat, sweating, and trembling, but the core and most consistently reported symptom is the subjective experience of overwhelming anxiety."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neuropsychological testing",
    "long_answer": "Cognitive deficits seen in antisocial personality disorder are typically evaluated with formal neuropsychological testing—standardized tasks such as the Wisconsin Card Sorting Test or other executive-function batteries that objectively measure attention, planning, impulse control and other frontal-lobe functions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Denker’s operation",
    "long_answer": "Denker’s operation (extended anterior-medial maxillectomy) is started with a sublabial incision that is carried through the maxillo-zygomatic buttress and continued medially to the inferior orbital rim. By removing the anterior and medial walls of the maxilla and part of the inferior turbinate, it fashions a very large common opening between the maxillary sinus and the midline nasal cavity, giving the surgeon a wide median nasal space."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mycology",
    "long_answer": "Mycology is the branch of biology devoted to the study of fungi—including the Entomophthorales species responsible for the disease entomophthoramycosis. It examines their taxonomy, physiology, ecology, and pathogenic mechanisms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "≈1 in 2,500 births",
    "long_answer": "Data from the British Columbia Newborn Screening Program show that roughly 0.04 % of screened infants are confirmed to have a treatable inborn error of metabolism, corresponding to an incidence of about one case per 2,500 live births."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Downward-slanting palpebral fissures",
    "long_answer": "A characteristic ocular feature of Nager (acrofacial dysostosis) syndrome is downward-slanting palpebral fissures—the outer corners of the eyelids sit lower than the inner corners, giving the eyes a slanted appearance. This finding is reported in the majority of affected individuals and is routinely used as a clinical clue for diagnosing the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Liver transplantation",
    "long_answer": "In cerebrotendinous xanthomatosis (CTX), the underlying defect is in the liver-expressed enzyme sterol 27-hydroxylase (CYP27A1), causing abnormal bile-acid synthesis and systemic cholestanol accumulation. Replacing the diseased liver with a donor liver through orthotopic liver transplantation provides a source of normal CYP27A1 activity, correcting bile-acid metabolism and halting further cholestanol deposition, so liver transplantation is considered a potential curative treatment option for CTX in selected cases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatologist",
    "long_answer": "Condylomata lata, the broad-based wart-like lesions of secondary syphilis, are manifestations of a cutaneous sexually transmitted infection. Their diagnosis and treatment fall within the specialty of dermatology (often combined with venereology/sexual health), so a dermatologist is the appropriate specialist to manage them."
}

{
    "short_answer": "B3GALTL",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the B3GALTL gene (also known as B3GLCT) are the established cause of autosomal-recessive Peters-plus syndrome. Independent studies have shown that loss-of-function mutations in the same gene can also present primarily as primary congenital glaucoma, linking B3GALTL defects to both disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LGMD type 1C",
    "long_answer": "Autosomal-dominant limb-girdle muscular dystrophy type 1C, caused by caveolin-3 deficiency, is characterized by early muscle hypertrophy (especially of calves and shoulder girdle) that masks weakness and gives patients a strikingly muscular or ‘pseudoathletic’ appearance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LYST gene mutation",
    "long_answer": "Chédiak–Higashi syndrome is an autosomal-recessive disorder produced by loss-of-function mutations in the LYST (lysosomal trafficking regulator) gene. Defective LYST disrupts lysosomal fission and trafficking, causing accumulation of giant granules in macrophages and other cells; this leads to infiltration and enlargement of reticuloendothelial organs such as the liver, manifesting clinically as hepatomegaly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Orthopedics",
    "long_answer": "Brachydactyly is a congenital skeletal anomaly characterized by abnormally short fingers or toes. Disorders of bone growth and limb structure are typically assessed and managed by orthopedic specialists, who have training in diagnosing skeletal malformations and performing corrective procedures when needed."
}

{
    "short_answer": "−α3.7 deletion",
    "long_answer": "Molecular surveys of the Negev Bedouin population have repeatedly shown that the single-gene −α3.7 deletion in the α-globin cluster is by far the most common mutation detected. This deletion removes 3.7 kb spanning one α-globin gene, producing the characteristic α-thalassaemia trait seen at very high carrier frequencies in this endogamous, highly consanguineous community."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Medical genetics",
    "long_answer": "Persistent Müllerian duct syndrome is an inherited disorder of sexual development caused by mutations in the anti-Müllerian hormone (AMH) gene or its receptor. Because it is fundamentally a genetic condition, diagnosis, counseling, and coordination of management are handled within the medical genetics specialty."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Aseptic aspiration",
    "long_answer": "Galactocele is treated by evacuating the retained milk with a wide-bore needle. To avoid converting the sterile cyst into an infected breast abscess, the needle puncture and drainage are carried out under strict aseptic (sterile) technique, i.e., aseptic aspiration, sometimes followed by instillation of an antiseptic or antibiotic solution."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Endocrinology",
    "long_answer": "Ornithinaemia (hyper-ornithinemia) is a metabolic disorder involving excess ornithine in the blood, usually due to inborn errors of the urea-cycle or amino-acid metabolism. Disorders of intermediary metabolism are classically evaluated and managed by endocrinology/metabolic specialists, who diagnose the enzymatic defect, monitor plasma amino-acid levels and institute dietary or pharmacologic therapy. Hence, the medical specialty most appropriate for diagnosing and treating ornithinaemia is endocrinology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Exfoliative erythroderma",
    "long_answer": "Pemphigus foliaceus, although initially presenting with superficial crusted erosions, can rapidly extend to involve almost the entire cutaneous surface, leading to diffuse erythema and scaling known as exfoliative erythroderma (generalised exfoliative dermatitis). This widespread inflammation and desquamation represent a severe, potentially life-threatening dermatologic state."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurology",
    "long_answer": "Neurofibromatoses are neuro-cutaneous genetic disorders characterized by tumors of peripheral and central nervous tissue; their evaluation, genetic confirmation, imaging surveillance, and medical or surgical management are classically overseen by neurologists, who coordinate care with genetics, dermatology, and neurosurgery as needed. Thus a neurology specialist would diagnose and treat Neurofibromatosis type 4."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ophthalmologist",
    "long_answer": "Ankyloblepharon is an adhesion of the eyelid margins that can impair ocular function and vision. Disorders of the eyelids and ocular adnexa are diagnosed and surgically corrected by an ophthalmologist (often an oculoplastic-trained ophthalmic surgeon). Therefore, an ophthalmologist is the specialist responsible for treating ankyloblepharon."
}

{
    "short_answer": "A markedly elevated MCHC",
    "long_answer": "During a routine automated complete blood count (CBC), red-cell agglutination caused by cold-reactive IgM antibodies makes the analyzer underestimate the number of erythrocytes while hemoglobin is measured correctly. The calculation therefore yields an unexpectedly high mean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration (MCHC, often >36 g/dL). This spurious elevation is a classic laboratory clue that prompts suspicion of cold agglutinin disease before specific testing is performed."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CHD7",
    "long_answer": "Lubani-Al-Saleh-Teebi syndrome represents the same clinical entity now recognised as CHARGE syndrome. Pathogenic variants in the chromodomain-helicase-DNA-binding gene CHD7 on chromosome 8q12 disrupt chromatin remodelling during embryogenesis and are responsible for the multisystem malformations (ocular coloboma, congenital heart defects, choanal atresia, ear anomalies, growth and genital defects) that characterise this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "C20H22N8O5",
    "long_answer": "Severe, treatment-resistant psoriasis is often managed systemically with the antimetabolite drug methotrexate. Methotrexate is an analogue of folic acid that inhibits dihydrofolate reductase, thereby reducing DNA synthesis in rapidly proliferating keratinocytes. The chemical formula of methotrexate is C20H22N8O5."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Otolaryngology (ENT)",
    "long_answer": "Madelung’s disease—also called multiple symmetric lipomatosis—produces large, symmetric fatty masses chiefly in the neck and upper thorax. These deposits can obstruct or distort airway and cervical structures, so definitive management relies on surgical excision or debulking. Such head-and-neck surgery is most commonly performed by otolaryngologists, making Otolaryngology (ENT) the specialty that typically treats patients with Madelung’s disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ketogenic diet",
    "long_answer": "For mitochondrial disorders—especially those that present with epilepsy that proves tolerant or refractory to antiepileptic drugs—a ketogenic (high-fat, low-carbohydrate) diet has been proposed. Ketone bodies supply acetyl-CoA and NADH while bypassing complex-I defects, improving mitochondrial ATP generation and reducing reactive oxygen species, and clinical reports show it can decrease seizure frequency in drug-resistant cases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mesoderm",
    "long_answer": "Poland’s syndrome features hypoplasia or absence of the pectoralis major muscle, a skeletal muscle that arises from paraxial (somitic) mesoderm. Thus, the germ layer implicated in these pectoral muscle abnormalities is the mesoderm."
}

{
    "short_answer": "B lymphocytes",
    "long_answer": "Osteoprotegerin (OPG, TNFRSF11B) is a soluble decoy receptor for RANKL that is produced not only by bone-forming osteoblasts but also by immune cells. Among haematopoietic lineages, mature B cells are the principal source: resting and activated B lymphocytes transcribe and secrete OPG, and B-cell-deficient mice display markedly reduced bone-marrow OPG levels and consequent bone loss, which can be reversed by reconstitution with B cells. Thus, the immune cell type that expresses (and secretes) the cytokine receptor osteoprotegerin is the B-cell lineage of lymphocytes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Medical genetics",
    "long_answer": "Homocystinuria is an autosomal-recessive inborn error of methionine metabolism caused most often by cystathionine-β-synthase deficiency. Because it is a genetic metabolic disorder, optimal care—including biochemical diagnosis, dietary therapy, vitamin/cofactor trials, and family genetic counseling—is coordinated by the medical genetics (inborn errors of metabolism) specialty."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Medical genetics",
    "long_answer": "Schneckenbecken dysplasia is a rare, usually lethal skeletal dysplasia caused by biallelic mutations in SLC35D1. Diagnosis relies on genetic testing and prenatal or neonatal imaging, and counseling about recurrence risk and pregnancy management is central. These activities—molecular diagnosis, interpretation of skeletal dysplasia findings, and genetic counseling—fall within the expertise of the medical genetics specialty."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fibroblasts",
    "long_answer": "In trigger finger, the fibroblasts that normally populate the flexor tendon pulley undergo a chondroid metaplasia, meaning they transform so that they resemble and function like cartilage-forming chondrocytes. Histologic studies of the thickened A1 pulley show these fibroblasts acquiring rounded morphology and expressing cartilage-associated matrix proteins, demonstrating their shift toward a cartilage-like phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "None",
    "long_answer": "The phosphoglucomutase reaction is an intramolecular transfer of the phosphate group between the 1- and 6-positions of glucose; it proceeds through a glucose-1,6-bisphosphate intermediate without cleavage of a bond by water. Therefore hydrolysis does not participate in, nor is it required for, the step catalyzed by phosphoglucomutase."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mouse Genome Informatics (MGI)",
    "long_answer": "The Mouse Genome Informatics (MGI) database—specifically its Mouse Genome Database (MGD) component—is the authoritative resource for curated information on mouse genes, alleles, and phenotypes. It catalogs all known mutations in the Suox gene that lead to sulfite oxidase deficiency in mice, providing genotype–phenotype annotations, strain data, and references, making it the appropriate database for this genetic defect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FERMT1 (KIND1)",
    "long_answer": "The rarest form of inherited epidermolysis bullosa is Kindler syndrome (also called Kindler type EB). This disorder is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the FERMT1 (KIND1) gene, which encodes the focal-adhesion protein kindlin-1 that is essential for dermal–epidermal adhesion. Defective kindlin-1 disrupts integrin activation, leading to the skin fragility and photosensitivity characteristic of this very uncommon EB subtype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nantucket Island",
    "long_answer": "Nantucket Island, off the coast of Massachusetts, is notorious for its high incidence of human babesiosis caused by the protozoan parasite Babesia microti; the disease was first recognized there in the 1960s and is sometimes called “Nantucket fever,” reflecting its historically elevated case rates compared with other U.S. locations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FKBP10",
    "long_answer": "Al-Gazali-Sabrinathan-Nair syndrome is another name for Bruck syndrome type 1, a disorder characterised by congenital joint contractures and bone fragility. Biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the FKBP10 gene, which encodes the endoplasmic-reticulum chaperone FKBP65, are responsible for this syndrome. The same FKBP10 mutations are also a recognised cause of autosomal-recessive osteogenesis imperfecta (classified as OI type XI). Thus, FKBP10 is the gene whose mutation is shared by both Al-Gazali Sabrinathan Nair syndrome and osteogenesis imperfecta."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hematology",
    "long_answer": "Myeloid leukemia is a malignancy of the blood-forming (myeloid) cells. Its diagnosis and management— including bone-marrow biopsies, cytogenetic testing, chemotherapy, targeted agents such as tyrosine-kinase inhibitors, and stem-cell transplantation—fall under hematology, the medical specialty devoted to diseases of blood and bone marrow (often practiced as hematology-oncology)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "His–Purkinje system",
    "long_answer": "In hypertrophic cardiomyopathy the interventricular septum becomes markedly hypertrophied and the myocytes in this region show architectural disarray. The His bundle and its right- and left-bundle branches (the His–Purkinje conduction system) course through this septum; their fibers are distorted and infiltrated by the hypertrophic, disorganized myocardium. This structural involvement of the His–Purkinje network underlies the intraventricular conduction delays and malignant ventricular tachyarrhythmias characteristic of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Zona fasciculata",
    "long_answer": "Glucocorticoid deficiency type 1 involves a marked lack of cortisol, the main glucocorticoid. Cortisol is synthesized predominantly in the middle layer of the adrenal cortex, the zona fasciculata, which is therefore the zone chiefly responsible for producing the hormone that is deficient in this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mandibular distraction osteogenesis",
    "long_answer": "Agnathia involves severe absence or hypoplasia of the mandible. The procedure now increasingly used to address this defect is mandibular distraction osteogenesis, in which controlled gradual separation of a surgically created osteotomy stimulates new bone formation, lengthening and reconstructing the mandible while simultaneously improving airway patency and facial symmetry."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vertical transmission",
    "long_answer": "In pediatric cases, HIV—and thus the risk for HIV-associated neurocognitive disorders—is most often acquired from mother to child during pregnancy, delivery, or breastfeeding, a route collectively termed vertical (perinatal) transmission."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autonomic nervous system disorder",
    "long_answer": "Congenital central hypoventilation syndrome is caused by mutations in the PHOX2B gene that disrupt the brainstem’s automatic control of breathing and other involuntary functions. Because these defects lie in autonomic regulation rather than the lungs themselves, CCHS is classified as a disorder of the autonomic nervous system (a form of dysautonomia)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hematoxylin-eosin stain",
    "long_answer": "The cutaneous hamartomas seen in Birt–Hogg–Dubé syndrome—fibrofolliculomas and trichodiscomas—are diagnosed histologically on routine paraffin sections stained with hematoxylin and eosin (H&E). This standard stain clearly shows the characteristic thin, anastomosing cords of follicular epithelium set within a fibrous perifollicular stroma, allowing definitive identification of these BHD-associated skin tumors."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sublingual",
    "long_answer": "In cyclic vomiting syndrome, benzodiazepines such as lorazepam and alprazolam are typically given sublingually—placed under the tongue—so they are absorbed through the oral mucosa. This route bypasses swallowing, provides rapid systemic absorption, and is practical when persistent vomiting makes oral dosing unreliable."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ERCC6 (CSB)",
    "long_answer": "Cockayne syndrome is most often caused by loss-of-function mutations in the ERCC6 gene, which encodes the CSB protein required for transcription-coupled nucleotide-excision DNA repair. Several reported patients with ERCC6 mutations have developed marked tubulo-interstitial renal damage and progressive loss of kidney function, culminating in end-stage renal disease. Hence, an ERCC6 (CSB) gene mutation can produce the classic features of Cockayne syndrome and, in some cases, lead to kidney failure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PRKAG2",
    "long_answer": "PRKAG2, located on chromosome 7q36, encodes the γ2 regulatory subunit of AMP-activated protein kinase (AMPK). Pathogenic missense mutations in PRKAG2 cause a familial syndrome that manifests as hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, frequently accompanied by ventricular pre-excitation and glycogen accumulation in cardiomyocytes. Thus, PRKAG2 mutations are a well-established chromosomal-7 cause of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rapp-Hodgkin syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Rapp-Hodgkin syndrome is an autosomal-dominant ectodermal dysplasia caused by TP63 mutations. Its clinical picture includes limb malformations such as partial syndactyly together with sparse, coarse scalp hair. Microscopic examination of the hair shaft shows marked (‘exaggerated’) pili torti, making this syndrome the recognized condition that combines syndactyly with pronounced pili torti."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Missense (glycine-substitution) mutation",
    "long_answer": "Severe and lethal osteogenesis imperfecta (types II and III) most often result from missense point mutations in the COL1A1 or COL1A2 genes that replace the obligatory glycine residues in the Gly-X-Y repeats of type I collagen. These glycine-substitution mutations disrupt the triple-helix structure, producing structurally abnormal collagen that exerts a dominant-negative effect, leading to the severe clinical phenotypes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dysphagia",
    "long_answer": "Cerebellar degeneration often impairs coordination of the oropharyngeal muscles, producing dysphagia (difficulty swallowing). When swallowing is ineffective, food or liquids can enter the airway, predisposing patients to aspiration pneumonia if the dysphagia is not managed."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SCNN1B",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic loss-of-function mutations in SCNN1B, the gene that encodes the β-subunit of the epithelial sodium channel (ENaC), cause the systemic form of Pseudohypoaldosteronism type 1. ENaC is also expressed in the uterine and endometrial epithelium, where its sodium-transport activity is crucial for fluid absorption and embryo implantation. Consequently, women with SCNN1B mutations can have defective endometrial receptivity and experience primary infertility in addition to the salt-wasting features of PHA-1."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mosaicism",
    "long_answer": "Although Adams–Oliver syndrome is usually inherited as a germ-line autosomal-dominant or autosomal-recessive trait, several documented patients (and some transmitting parents) carry the pathogenic variant only in a subset of their cells. This somatic or gonadal mosaicism is a recognized but rare genetic phenomenon associated with Adams–Oliver syndrome and explains otherwise sporadic or variably expressed cases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Iatrogenic",
    "long_answer": "When a pleural effusion arises as a direct consequence of medical or surgical intervention—such as thoracic surgery, central line placement, or drug therapy—the cause is termed 'iatrogenic.' The adjective denotes any condition induced unintentionally by diagnostic or therapeutic procedures performed by healthcare professionals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "The MYO7A gene, which encodes the motor protein myosin VIIA and whose mutations cause Usher syndrome type I (specifically the USH1B subtype) as well as some forms of non-syndromic deafness, is located on chromosome 11 (cytogenetic band 11q13.5). It is not situated on chromosome 16. Therefore, the statement that MYO7A is found on chromosome 16 is incorrect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "college",
    "long_answer": "In media profiles of one of the oldest known survivors of the extremely rare over-growth disorder MOMO syndrome, the teenager states that his goal for the future is to go to college. This aspiration—pursuing higher education—has been repeatedly highlighted as a milestone he hopes to achieve despite the medical challenges associated with the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FLNA",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the X-linked gene FLNA (filamin A) are a recognised cause of chronic intestinal pseudo-obstruction.  Filamin A is an actin-binding cytoskeletal protein that interacts with guidance receptors such as ROBO and netrin receptors, helping to steer axon growth cones.  Through these interactions FLNA is required for proper motor-neuron outgrowth and positioning during embryonic development, placing the gene in the Gene Ontology category “motor neuron development.”"
}

{
    "short_answer": "Orthopedics",
    "long_answer": "Split hand split foot-nystagmus syndrome is characterized mainly by ectrodactyly (split hand/foot malformation). Corrective surgery, prosthetic fitting, and long-term functional care for these complex limb anomalies are undertaken by orthopedic surgeons, usually hand- and foot-specialized orthopedists. While ophthalmology may address the associated nystagmus, the core therapeutic management of the syndrome’s hallmark skeletal defects lies within the orthopedic specialty."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pulmonology",
    "long_answer": "Chronic mountain sickness (Monge’s disease) results from prolonged hypobaric hypoxia at high altitude, leading to ventilatory impairment, severe polycythemia, pulmonary hypertension and right-sided heart strain. Management focuses on improving oxygenation (descent, supplemental O₂, acetazolamide) and controlling related pulmonary complications, all of which fall under the expertise of pulmonology/respiratory medicine specialists."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Medical genetics",
    "long_answer": "Trisomy 9 is a chromosomal aneuploidy; its confirmation requires cytogenetic testing and its care plan involves evaluation of multisystem anomalies and genetic counseling. These activities fall within the purview of the medical genetics specialty, which is responsible for diagnosing chromosomal disorders and guiding their clinical management."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Coronary arteries",
    "long_answer": "The gravest complication of eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis is cardiac involvement, most notably necrotising vasculitis of the coronary arteries. Coronary arteritis can lead to myocarditis, heart failure, and fatal arrhythmias, making these arteries the critical vessels affected in the disease’s most serious manifestation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Aplasia",
    "long_answer": "Aplasia is the medical term for the complete failure of an organ or tissue to develop during embryogenesis. In disorders such as Larsen syndrome, various structures—including bones, ligaments, or other organs—may be absent because they are aplastic, meaning they never formed in the first place."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ZIC3 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the X-linked ZIC3 gene are a recognized cause of heterotaxy with atrial isomerism. Because the trait is X-linked, a carrier mother has a 50 % chance of passing the mutation to each pregnancy; affected male fetuses frequently develop atrial isomerism and complex congenital heart disease. Identifying a ZIC3 mutation therefore provides critical information for genetic counselling, recurrence-risk assessment, and targeted prenatal or pre-implantation testing in the family."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tracheostomy",
    "long_answer": "In recurrent respiratory (laryngeal) papillomatosis, repeated endoscopic debulking or laser excisions are the standard treatment. When papillomas recur so rapidly that airway obstruction persists despite multiple debulkings, a tracheostomy can be performed to bypass the obstructed laryngeal airway and secure long-term ventilation. Although tracheostomy is avoided when possible because it may facilitate distal spread of papillomas, it remains the definitive surgical option for severe, refractory cases where other surgical and adjuvant measures have failed."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Otitis media",
    "long_answer": "Clinical surveys of 48,XXYY males show a high rate of recurrent or chronic otitis media (middle-ear infections). Craniofacial differences and eustachian-tube dysfunction in this aneuploidy increase the likelihood of fluid retention and infection in the middle ear, making otitis media a common ear problem in individuals with XXYY syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LYST",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the LYST (lysosomal trafficking regulator) gene are the classical cause of Chédiak–Higashi syndrome.  Because LYST is essential for proper maturation and transport of melanosomes in melanocytes, certain LYST mutations also give rise to pigmentary abnormalities and sensorineural deafness that meet clinical criteria for Waardenburg syndrome type 2.  Thus, mutation of the single gene LYST underlies both Chédiak–Higashi syndrome and Waardenburg syndrome type 2."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lateral pterygoid",
    "long_answer": "In Meige’s syndrome, dystonic activity frequently involves the muscles that move the mandible. Bilateral involuntary contraction of the lateral pterygoid muscles pulls the mandible anteriorly, producing protrusion of the jaw so that the chin is thrust forward."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Anticholinergic medications",
    "long_answer": "Drugs with anticholinergic activity (such as many antihistamines, tricyclic antidepressants, antispasmodics) block muscarinic parasympathetic stimulation of the salivary glands, markedly reducing saliva flow (xerostomia). The resulting salivary stasis predisposes the parotid or submandibular glands to ascending bacterial infection and inflammation, producing sialadenitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neuronal NAA synthesis",
    "long_answer": "In Canavan’s disease, N-acetylaspartate (NAA) is made inside neuronal mitochondria. Because aspartoacylase is deficient, the NAA that is synthesized in mitochondria cannot be degraded in oligodendrocytes, so it accumulates excessively and the brain is deprived of the acetate needed for myelin lipid production. Thus, mitochondrial production of NAA is the upstream metabolic step that drives the pathological build-up of NAA and contributes to the dysmyelination characteristic of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "KRIT1 (CCM1)",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the KRIT1 gene, also called CCM1, are a well-established cause of familial cerebral cavernous hemangiomas. Experimental deletion of Krit1 in mice disrupts endothelial-cell junctions and vascular morphogenesis, leading to severe hemorrhage and death during mid-gestation, demonstrating that loss of this gene is embryonically lethal in mice."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Medical genetics",
    "long_answer": "Pipecolic acidemia is an inborn error of metabolism caused by a hereditary defect in peroxisomal pipecolic acid oxidation. Disorders of this type are evaluated through specialized metabolic testing and molecular genetic analysis, and their ongoing management (including genetic counseling, dietary or enzyme-based therapies, and monitoring for neurological sequelae) is overseen by physicians trained in medical genetics/biochemical genetics. Therefore, the medical genetics specialty is the one that typically diagnoses and treats pipecolic acidemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "In the families described with Corneal-cerebellar syndrome, the disorder appears only when both copies of the causal gene are mutated, while clinically normal parents transmit the mutation to affected offspring. This carrier-parent/affected-child pattern is the hallmark of autosomal-recessive inheritance, which is the accepted mode of transmission for Corneal-cerebellar syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Misoprostol",
    "long_answer": "Misoprostol, a synthetic prostaglandin E1 analogue, is sometimes used topically (e.g., as a mouth-rinse or tablet applied to the lesion) to promote healing and pain relief in recurrent aphthous stomatitis. Systemically, the same drug is well established in obstetrics for cervical ripening and stimulation of uterine contractions, making it a standard agent for induction of labor and medical termination of pregnancy. Thus, misoprostol links the two clinical uses referenced in the question."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ZSWIM6",
    "long_answer": "The syndrome known as Trigonocephaly-bifid nose-acral anomalies (also called acromelic frontonasal dysostosis) has been shown to result from heterozygous missense mutations in the ZSWIM6 gene. Pathogenic variants such as p.Arg1295Leu in ZSWIM6 disrupt embryonic craniofacial and limb development, producing the characteristic combination of metopic craniosynostosis (trigonocephaly), bifid or broad nasal tip, and distal limb (acral) anomalies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RBPJ",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in RBPJ (Recombination Signal Binding Protein for Immunoglobulin κ J region) cause Adams–Oliver syndrome type 2. Independent genome-wide association and candidate-gene studies have also identified common and rare RBPJ variants as risk factors for nonsyndromic cleft lip with or without cleft palate, linking this Notch-pathway transcription factor to both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurologist",
    "long_answer": "NARP syndrome (Neuropathy, Ataxia, and Retinitis Pigmentosa) primarily affects the nervous system—peripheral nerves, cerebellar pathways, and sometimes the visual pathways—making its diagnosis and management the domain of a neurologist, the specialist trained to evaluate, diagnose, and treat neurological disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Olfactophobia",
    "long_answer": "The term for an excessive, irrational fear of odors is olfactophobia. It combines the Latin stem \"olfacto-\" (from olfacere, “to smell”) with the Greek suffix \"-phobia\" (fear). Clinically, people with olfactophobia experience marked anxiety or avoidance behaviors when exposed to certain smells, distinguishing it from the more commonly cited Greek-derived term “osmophobia.”"
}

{
    "short_answer": "Median mandibular cleft",
    "long_answer": "The median (midline) cleft of the mandible and lower lip (Tessier no. 30) is a rare congenital malformation produced by failure of fusion of the paired mandibular prominences. Because this fusion defect leaves part of the tongue tethered to the floor of the mouth, an abnormally short or persistent frenulum linguae (ankyloglossia) is almost always present. Thus the malformation simultaneously involves a midline facial cleft and an abnormal frenulum linguae."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Enterovirus infection",
    "long_answer": "Non-polio enteroviruses, particularly Coxsackie A and B viruses, are well-documented causes of acute viral gastroenteritis in children. The same enteroviruses have repeatedly been identified as precipitating infections in patients who later develop myalgic encephalomyelitis/chronic fatigue syndrome (ME/CFS), with studies showing elevated enteroviral RNA or antibodies in ME/CFS cohorts. Thus, enterovirus infection is a recognized common trigger for both pediatric gastroenteritis and the onset of ME/CFS."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FUS",
    "long_answer": "The FUS (fused in sarcoma) gene, mapped to chromosome 16p11.2, encodes an RNA-binding protein. Pathogenic FUS mutations are a well-established cause of autosomal dominant frontotemporal dementia that can present with parkinsonism, linking this chromosome-16 gene to the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NKX2-5 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous pathogenic variants in the NKX2-5 homeobox transcription factor gene are a well-established cause of familial secundum atrial septal defects. NKX2-5 directs cardiac septation during embryogenesis, and loss-of-function mutations disrupt this process, leading to ASDs (often with atrioventricular conduction block). These variants are routinely detectable by clinical cardiac gene panels or exome sequencing, making NKX2-5 mutation the canonical genetic defect identified through testing in patients with atrial septal defects."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Clindamycin",
    "long_answer": "Clindamycin is a lincosamide antibiotic routinely prescribed for bacterial infections. Topical clindamycin has also been reported to lessen the pruritic papules of Fox-Fordyce disease by reducing inflammation and bacterial colonization in the affected apocrine follicles."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CLN3 gene",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the CLN3 gene (responsible for the classic juvenile form of neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis, or Batten disease) not only produce progressive neurodegeneration but also lead to lysosomal storage within cardiomyocytes, causing hypertrophic changes, conduction abnormalities, and other myocardial pathology. Thus, the CLN3 gene mutation is the NCL-associated defect known to affect the myocardium."
}

{
    "short_answer": "2022",
    "long_answer": "In the International Classification of Diseases, Asperger syndrome remained a distinct diagnosis through ICD-10. It was subsumed under the single category “autism spectrum disorder” in the next revision, ICD-11, which officially came into effect on 1 January 2022; from that date the separate Asperger syndrome code ceased to exist."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Preservation of vision",
    "long_answer": "Gliomas of the second cranial (optic) nerve are generally low-grade tumors that rarely threaten life, but they can rapidly impair visual function. Therefore, therapeutic decisions (observation, chemotherapy, limited surgery or radiotherapy) are guided mainly by eyesight considerations. The overarching, widely accepted goal is to maintain or maximize the patient’s remaining visual acuity rather than to achieve total tumor eradication."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Poor intracellular penetration",
    "long_answer": "Legionella pneumophila survives and multiplies inside alveolar macrophages. Aminoglycoside antibiotics do not enter eukaryotic cells efficiently, so therapeutic concentrations are not achieved within these infected macrophages. Because of this poor intracellular penetration, aminoglycosides cannot reliably reach or kill the intracellular Legionella organisms, making them ineffective and therefore not recommended for treating Legionnaires' disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Type IV hypersensitivity",
    "long_answer": "AIDS selectively destroys CD4⁺ T-helper cells, crippling cell-mediated immunity. Because Type IV (delayed-type, T-cell-mediated) hypersensitivity reactions require intact CD4⁺ Th1 cells to activate macrophages and cytotoxic T lymphocytes, this class of hypersensitivity is markedly impaired in patients with AIDS."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sulfatides and glycosaminoglycans",
    "long_answer": "Multiple sulfatase deficiency results from loss of activity of all lysosomal sulfatase enzymes. Without these enzymes, sulfate groups cannot be removed from their substrates, so the sulfated sphingolipid sulfatides and the sulfated polysaccharides (glycosaminoglycans such as heparan and dermatan sulfate) are not degraded and progressively build up inside lysosomes, producing the characteristic lysosomal storage pathology of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vitamin C deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Epidemiological and metabolic studies show that inadequate vitamin C (ascorbic acid) intake raises gallstone risk. Vitamin C is a required co-factor for cholesterol 7-α-hydroxylase, the enzyme that converts cholesterol into bile acids. When vitamin C is lacking, bile acid synthesis falls, bile becomes supersaturated with cholesterol, and cholesterol precipitates to form gallstones. Populations with low plasma vitamin C or dietary intake consistently exhibit a higher prevalence of cholesterol gallstones."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Disseminated intravascular coagulation",
    "long_answer": "During complicated delivery or septic/unsafe abortion, large amounts of tissue factor enter the maternal circulation, triggering disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC). The widespread activation of the coagulation cascade causes micro-vascular thrombosis, leading to ischemia and tissue necrosis while simultaneously consuming clotting factors and platelets."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Risk of iatrogenic fractures",
    "long_answer": "In osteogenesis imperfecta the bones are extremely fragile; the weight and leverage of a hip spica cast can place excessive stress on adjacent brittle bones, often causing new (iatrogenic) fractures during application or while the cast is in place. To avoid provoking additional fractures, lighter immobilization or internal fixation is preferred over a hip spica cast in these patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PGM1",
    "long_answer": "Phosphoglucomutase-1 (PGM1) was originally labelled as the cause of glycogen storage disease type XIV, because loss of its activity disturbs the interconversion of glucose-1-phosphate and glucose-6-phosphate and leads to abnormal glycogen handling. Subsequent work showed that the same block critically limits formation of UDP-glucose and UDP-galactose, precursors needed to build N-linked glycans. Because the dominant clinical mechanism proved to be a defect in protein N-glycosylation, the condition was re-classified as PGM1-CDG (a congenital disorder of glycosylation) and the gene PGM1 was de-listed as a classical glycogen storage disease gene."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dodecamer repeat expansion",
    "long_answer": "Unverricht–Lundborg disease (progressive myoclonus epilepsy type 1) is driven by an unstable polymeric array of a 12-base-pair minisatellite (dodecamer) in the promoter of the CSTB (cystatin B) gene. Pathogenic expansion of this dodecamer repeat reduces CSTB expression, and the size of the repeat polymer correlates with disease onset and severity, making the dodecamer repeat expansion the key polymeric structure in disease progression."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Spirochaetaceae",
    "long_answer": "Treponema pallidum, the spirochete responsible for human syphilis, is a species in the genus Treponema. This genus is classified within the family Spirochaetaceae, one of the four recognized families in the order Spirochaetales. Therefore, the family that includes the syphilis pathogen is Spirochaetaceae."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hyperlipidemia",
    "long_answer": "In Glycogen storage disease type VI (Hers disease), defective hepatic glycogen phosphorylase limits glucose release during fasting. The body compensates with increased lipolysis and ketogenesis, which can chronically elevate plasma triglycerides and cholesterol. This persistent hyperlipidemia is a recognized complication of GSD VI and often necessitates ongoing lipid-lowering medication to keep blood lipid levels within a safe range."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Carbamazepine",
    "long_answer": "Carbamazepine, an anticonvulsant that enhances the release and action of endogenous vasopressin, can reduce polyuria and polydipsia in patients with central diabetes insipidus and is therefore sometimes used as adjunct or alternative therapy when desmopressin is not suitable."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Variola sine eruptione",
    "long_answer": "In classical descriptions of smallpox, the febrile prodrome is normally followed by the characteristic vesicular-pustular skin eruption. Rarely, infection produces only the prodromal fever and toxemia without any cutaneous lesions; such rash-less cases were historically termed “variola sine eruptione,” literally ‘smallpox without eruption.’"
}

{
    "short_answer": "Psycholinguistic testing",
    "long_answer": "Foix–Chavany–Marie (opercular) syndrome is confirmed not only with neuro-imaging but also by detailed language evaluation; clinicians perform psycholinguistic testing, which examines how linguistic abilities relate to underlying cognitive and psychological processes. This form of assessment distinguishes the voluntary speech impairment typical of the syndrome from primary aphasic disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "The characteristic cytogenetic abnormality of follicular lymphoma is the t(14;18)(q32;q21) translocation, which juxtaposes the BCL2 gene on chromosome 18 to the immunoglobulin heavy-chain locus on chromosome 14, leading to over-expression of BCL2 and inhibition of apoptosis. A t(16;18) translocation is not part of the established cytogenetic profile of follicular lymphoma and is not regarded as a defining or common alteration for this disease. Therefore, follicular lymphoma is not linked to t(16;18)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vitamin C deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Severe alcoholism is frequently accompanied by malnutrition, particularly a deficiency of vitamin C. Without adequate vitamin C, proline and lysine in collagen cannot be hydroxylated, preventing proper cross-linking of collagen fibers. The resulting weak connective tissue in the aortic media and adventitia cannot resist intraluminal pressure, so the aortic wall dilates and is more prone to form an aneurysm. Thus, alcoholism raises aortic aneurysm risk mainly through vitamin C–deficiency-mediated weakening of the vessel wall."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SH2B3 (LNK)",
    "long_answer": "Genome-wide association studies have repeatedly linked the SH2B3 gene, which encodes the adaptor protein LNK, to multiple complex traits. The nonsynonymous variant rs3184504 (R262W) in SH2B3 shows strong association with elevated systolic and diastolic blood pressure and therefore essential hypertension. The same variant is also one of the most replicated risk loci for autoimmune diseases, including type 1 diabetes, where altered SH2B3 signaling affects lymphocyte activation and tolerance. Thus, SH2B3 is a single gene convincingly associated with both essential hypertension and type 1 diabetes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PTCH1",
    "long_answer": "Germ-line loss-of-function mutations in the PTCH1 gene, which encodes the Patched-1 receptor in the Hedgehog signalling pathway, cause basal cell naevus (Gorlin–Goltz) syndrome. A hallmark of this syndrome is the development of multiple basal-cell carcinomas, and affected patients also characteristically form excessive wound-healing responses, leading to hypertrophic or keloid-type scars after even minor trauma or surgery. Thus the same PTCH1 mutation is linked to both basal-cell carcinoma and hypertrophic scarring."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Orthopedic surgery",
    "long_answer": "Kozlowski-Krajewska syndrome is another name for the Kozlowski type of spondylometaphyseal dysplasia—a hereditary skeletal dysplasia that causes abnormal development of the spine, long-bone metaphyses and epiphyses, short stature, coxa vara and other bone deformities. Management centers on monitoring growth, correcting limb and spinal deformities, and, when necessary, performing surgical procedures such as osteotomies or spinal fusion. These evaluations and interventions are carried out primarily by orthopedic surgeons (often pediatric orthopedists), making orthopedics the specialty most likely to handle treatment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Growth hormone",
    "long_answer": "Children with Cockayne syndrome often show pituitary growth-hormone (GH) deficiency, which contributes to their severe post-natal growth failure. Published case reports document catch-up growth when affected children were given recombinant human growth hormone. GH restores circulating IGF-1 and stimulates longitudinal bone growth, making it the hormone considered for therapeutic use in this setting."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ABCC8",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in ABCC8, which encodes the SUR1 subunit of the pancreatic β-cell KATP channel, can produce opposite functional effects. Activating mutations reduce insulin secretion and lead to transient neonatal diabetes mellitus, whereas inactivating (loss-of-function) mutations cause excessive insulin release and congenital hyperinsulinism. Thus the same gene, ABCC8, is well-established to underlie both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Manganese poisoning",
    "long_answer": "During steel manufacture, ferromanganese alloys are added and workers are chronically exposed to manganese-containing dusts and fumes. Inhalation of these aerosols can produce systemic manganese toxicity (“manganism”), an occupational disease characterized by neuro-behavioural and Parkinson-like features. Hence, manganese poisoning is the classic occupational disease linked to the iron and steel industry."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Methylmercury poisoning",
    "long_answer": "The first officially recognized Minamata-disease patient—a 5-year-old girl admitted on 21 April 1956—died from severe neurological damage produced by methylmercury poisoning. Industrial effluent from the Chisso chemical plant had contaminated fish and shellfish in Minamata Bay with methylmercury; ingestion of this neurotoxin led to irreversible central-nervous-system degeneration, respiratory failure, and ultimately death in the index case."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Distal ulna",
    "long_answer": "In amyoplasia-type arthrogryposis the wrist characteristically sits in flexion with marked ulnar deviation. This mal-alignment is produced by bowing and relative overgrowth of the distal ulna; corrective operations frequently include distal-ulnar shortening or wedge osteotomy. Thus, the bone primarily involved in the typical wrist deformity is the distal ulna."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Charolais cattle",
    "long_answer": "A naturally occurring form of glycogen storage disease type V has been documented in Charolais cattle. A recessive mutation in the PYGM gene abolishes muscle glycogen phosphorylase (myophosphorylase) activity, so affected Charolais animals lack this enzyme in skeletal muscle, providing the classic example of myophosphorylase deficiency in cattle."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TNF-α inhibitors",
    "long_answer": "Biologic agents that block tumour necrosis factor-alpha (TNF-α)—such as infliximab, etanercept and adalimumab—are established treatments for psoriatic arthritis. Because SAPHO syndrome shares a TNF-α-driven inflammatory mechanism, several case reports and small series have shown clinical and radiological improvement when patients with SAPHO were treated with these TNF-α inhibitors, so this drug class is often recommended when first-line therapies fail."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Intramuscular injection",
    "long_answer": "First-line therapy for many neuroendocrine tumors involves long-acting somatostatin analogs (e.g., octreotide LAR), which are routinely delivered as depot intramuscular injections every 4 weeks. This injection route provides sustained drug release, controls hormone-related symptoms, and slows tumor growth, making intramuscular administration a common treatment method in NET patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Oslo",
    "long_answer": "Jervell and Lange-Nielsen syndrome—a congenital long-QT syndrome associated with sensorineural deafness—was first documented in 1957 by Anton Jervell and Fred Lange-Nielsen in Oslo, Norway, where they described a Norwegian family exhibiting prolonged QT intervals, syncopal episodes, and congenital deaf-mutism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Adenocarcinoma",
    "long_answer": "Repeated mechanical and inflammatory irritation of the intestinal mucosa by retained enteroliths in diverticula has been reported to promote malignant transformation of the epithelial lining, with several case reports describing the development of adenocarcinoma arising within those chronically inflamed diverticula."
}

{
    "short_answer": "About 2–3 years",
    "long_answer": "In laboratory and wild populations, Mus musculus typically survives for roughly two years on average, with the oldest individuals rarely exceeding three years; mice beyond 18–24 months are therefore considered elderly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "XPC",
    "long_answer": "Melanoma risk is increased by germ-line mutations in the XPC gene. XPC encodes the DNA-damage-recognition factor of nucleotide-excision repair; when it is mutated, cells cannot efficiently repair UV-induced pyrimidine dimers, leading to accumulation of mutations and a strong predisposition to skin cancers such as melanoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MYO1A",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the MYO1A gene, located on human chromosome 12q12-q14, cause autosomal-dominant nonsyndromic sensorineural deafness (designated DFNA48). MYO1A encodes the unconventional motor protein myosin IA, which is essential for proper organization and tension of actin-rich stereocilia in the cochlear hair cells; disruption of this protein impairs mechano-electrical transduction and leads to isolated hearing loss without additional syndromic features."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MYH9 gene",
    "long_answer": "Polymorphisms in the MYH9 (non-muscle myosin heavy-chain 9) locus on chromosome 22 are a well-established genetic risk factor for a wide range of nondiabetic end-stage renal diseases—such as focal segmental glomerulosclerosis, HIV-associated nephropathy, and hypertension-attributed ESRD. In addition, pathogenic MYH9 mutations can produce glomerular involvement and platelet abnormalities that lead to microscopic or gross hematuria, linking this same locus to episodes of blood in the urine."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rhabdomyolysis",
    "long_answer": "Individuals with adenosine monophosphate deaminase deficiency type 1 already have impaired muscle energy metabolism; adding a statin further stresses skeletal‐muscle cells and markedly raises the danger of statin-induced myopathy progressing to rhabdomyolysis—acute, widespread muscle breakdown that can lead to kidney injury."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome is a congenital overgrowth disorder caused by altered imprinting of genes on chromosome 11p15. Clinical hallmarks such as prenatal and birth macrosomia, macroglossia and abdominal wall defects are evident in the newborn period, so the condition is usually detectable at birth. The syndrome is extensively documented in public-domain sources such as MedlinePlus, GARD and open-access literature."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CNS axon regeneration failure",
    "long_answer": "The optic nerve is composed of retinal ganglion-cell axons, which are part of the central nervous system (CNS). Unlike peripheral nerves, mature CNS axons lack the intrinsic growth programs and supportive glial environment needed for regrowth. After injury, oligodendrocyte-derived inhibitors (e.g., Nogo-A, MAG) and the glial scar prevent axonal extension, and the neurons themselves do not re-enter a regenerative state. Because these axons cannot reconnect to their targets in the brain, visual signals cannot be transmitted, making optic-nerve damage essentially permanent and blindness irreversible."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tremor",
    "long_answer": "Females with trisomy X (47,XXX) often exhibit a fine or intention tremor as part of their mild neurological and motor‐coordination difficulties. Tremor is likewise a cardinal motor manifestation of several neurological disorders, most famously Parkinson’s disease, where loss of dopaminergic neurons produces resting tremor. Thus, tremor is a symptom common to both trisomy X and neurological diseases such as Parkinson’s."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia congenita",
    "long_answer": "Spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia congenita (SEDc) is an inherited skeletal dysplasia caused by pathogenic variants in the COL2A1 gene encoding type II collagen. Defective collagen disrupts endochondral ossification, producing markedly flattened vertebral bodies (platyspondyly) throughout the spine and early-onset thoracic scoliosis/kyphosis. These characteristic spinal changes, often noted on neonatal radiographs, distinguish SEDc from other causes of spinal deformity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Keratin 1 (KRT1)",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the KRT1 gene, which encodes keratin 1, have been documented to cause both epidermolytic palmoplantar keratoderma (Vörner type, showing epidermolytic hyperkeratosis) and nonepidermolytic palmoplantar keratoderma (Unna-Thost type, lacking epidermolysis). Thus keratin 1 is uniquely linked to both histologic variants of palmoplantar keratoderma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bronchoscopic Nd:YAG laser ablation",
    "long_answer": "In symptomatic tracheobronchopathia osteochondroplastica, nodular bony/cartilaginous outgrowths can narrow the tracheobronchial lumen.  Endobronchial debulking with a neodymium-doped yttrium-aluminium-garnet (Nd:YAG) laser performed through a rigid or flexible bronchoscope is the standard therapeutic procedure to vaporise or core out the obstructing nodules and promptly restore airway patency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Staphylococcus aureus",
    "long_answer": "Granulomatosis with polyangiitis (GPA) has been linked to chronic nasal carriage of Staphylococcus aureus, which is thought to provide a persistent antigenic stimulus that may trigger or exacerbate the auto-immune response leading to ANCA production. Staphylococcus aureus is also a well-recognized, common bacterial cause of community- and hospital-acquired pneumonia, making it the pathogen that fulfills both aspects of the question."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mechanical ventilation",
    "long_answer": "Acute interstitial pneumonitis (Hamman–Rich syndrome) progresses rapidly to severe hypoxemic respiratory failure. No drug therapy has proven benefit; survival depends on aggressive supportive care. Endotracheal intubation with mechanical ventilation—usually delivered and monitored in an intensive care unit—is therefore regarded as the only consistently successful treatment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Aortic root",
    "long_answer": "Larsen syndrome can be accompanied by progressive dilation of the ascending aorta/aortic root, similar to that seen in other connective-tissue disorders. To slow aortic root enlargement and reduce wall stress, beta-blockade with propranolol is recommended, mirroring the strategy used in Marfan syndrome and related conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mepacrine (quinacrine)",
    "long_answer": "Platelets with normal dense granules actively take up the fluorescent antibiotic mepacrine (also called quinacrine). In δ-storage pool deficiency the dense granules are absent or reduced, so platelets fail to accumulate mepacrine, providing a simple diagnostic test for this form of platelet storage pool deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Decreased surface area-to-volume ratio",
    "long_answer": "In hereditary spherocytosis mutations in membrane-skeleton proteins (e.g., spectrin, ankyrin) cause loss of plasma-membrane fragments, so red cells become spherical instead of biconcave. The spherical shape has a smaller surface area relative to its volume, providing fewer membrane sites for diffusion of O₂ and CO₂. This reduced surface area-to-volume ratio limits the efficiency of gas exchange across the red-blood-cell membrane."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ARHGAP31",
    "long_answer": "ARHGAP31, which encodes a Rho-GTPase–activating protein, is a well-established autosomal-dominant cause of Adams–Oliver syndrome. In the reported testing, sequencing of ARHGAP31 was performed but no pathogenic or likely pathogenic variants were detected, indicating that this particular case of Adams–Oliver syndrome was not due to mutations in ARHGAP31."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chronic productive inflammation",
    "long_answer": "Banti’s syndrome (non-cirrhotic portal hypertension with congestive splenomegaly) shows a chronic productive, or proliferative, inflammation of the portal and splenic veins—an obliterative phlebitis in which fibrous tissue gradually replaces the vessel wall. This chronic productive inflammatory process underlies the vascular obstruction that characterises the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CECR2",
    "long_answer": "Cat-eye syndrome (Schmid-Fraccaro syndrome) is produced by partial trisomy/tetrasomy of the 22q11.2 segment. Within this duplicated segment, the dosage-sensitive gene CECR2 (Cat Eye Syndrome Chromosome Region, candidate 2) has been identified as a major contributor to the characteristic ocular coloboma and other developmental anomalies seen in the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Closed body language",
    "long_answer": "People with schizotypal personality disorder often hold a rigid, hunched or self-protective posture—arms crossed, limited gesturing, little eye contact. This closed body language non-verbally signals anxiety and social withdrawal, so observers interpret them as aloof or odd and tend to keep their distance. Consequently, the posture reduces opportunities for positive social engagement and reinforces the individual’s social isolation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Intra-aortic balloon pump",
    "long_answer": "An intra-aortic balloon pump (IABP) can be inserted percutaneously to provide temporary circulatory support in acute decompensated heart failure secondary to severe aortic stenosis. The IABP inflates during diastole to augment coronary perfusion and deflates just before systole to reduce left-ventricular afterload, thereby stabilizing hemodynamics until definitive aortic valve intervention can be performed."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lymphocytes",
    "long_answer": "In viral croup (laryngotracheobronchitis) the parainfluenza virus triggers a predominantly lymphocytic inflammatory response. These lymphocytes infiltrate the mucosa and submucosa of the larynx and trachea, leading to edema and the characteristic airway swelling and narrowing that produce the stridor and barking cough of croup."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Greek origin",
    "long_answer": "The term “encopresis” is derived from the Greek words “en” (in) and “kopros” (feces or dung), literally meaning ‘in feces.’ It was coined to describe the condition in which toilet-trained children aged four years or older involuntarily pass stool in inappropriate places."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Kapchorwa",
    "long_answer": "During the 2017 Marburg virus outbreak in eastern Uganda, a 38-year-old male health-care worker who had treated earlier patients began feeling ill on 5 November 2017 while in Kapchorwa town (Kapchorwa District). Laboratory testing later confirmed that his illness was Marburg virus disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MAF",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous loss- or gain-of-function mutations in the transcription factor gene MAF (v-maf musculoaponeurotic fibrosarcoma oncogene homolog) have been recurrently reported in families that present with the classic Cataract-microcornea syndrome. The same MAF mutations are also documented in patients who, in addition to cataract and microcornea, show peripheral sclerocornea, indicating that alteration of MAF is the genetic lesion common to both phenotypes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tramadol",
    "long_answer": "Tramadol is an atypical opioid analgesic frequently prescribed for peripheral (neuropathic) pain. It acts as a µ-opioid receptor agonist and inhibits reuptake of serotonin and norepinephrine. The opioid component can produce dose-dependent respiratory depression, while the serotonergic activity can precipitate serotonin syndrome, especially when combined with other serotonergic drugs. These dual mechanisms explain why tramadol both treats neuropathic pain and carries risks of respiratory depression and serotonin syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Children",
    "long_answer": "A small latent outward deviation of the eyes (exophoria) is frequently observed in childhood; most school-aged children exhibit a mild exophoria that they control with strong fusional convergence reserves, making this age group the one in which exophoria is particularly common."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cholesterol",
    "long_answer": "In Coats’ disease the yellow-white subretinal exudate is rich in lipid material, and microscopic examination shows numerous cholesterol crystals. Cholesterol is likewise liberated during degradation of red blood cells—the membrane lipids released as hemoglobin is broken down are converted to free cholesterol that can precipitate as crystals. Hence cholesterol is the common substance found both in Coats’ subretinal exudate and as a by-product of hemoglobin breakdown."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Coarse crackles",
    "long_answer": "In diffuse panbronchiolitis, inflammation and mucus accumulation in the small airways produce discontinuous, popping sounds during inspiration. These bilateral coarse crackles (rales) are readily heard on chest auscultation with a stethoscope and are a characteristic physical finding of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Physical therapy",
    "long_answer": "Children born with arthrogryposis multiplex congenita are routinely started on intensive physical therapy to stretch contracted joints, improve range of motion, and strengthen muscles. Early, ongoing physiotherapy is the cornerstone of non-surgical management and is recommended by standard clinical guidelines for this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Glassblower",
    "long_answer": "A laryngocele is an air-filled dilation of the laryngeal saccule that enlarges when intralaryngeal pressure is repeatedly raised. People who blow glass for a living generate sustained high expiratory pressures, and the condition has historically been nicknamed “glass-blower’s disease.” Therefore, the occupation classically linked to an increased risk of laryngocele is glassblowing."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurology",
    "long_answer": "Foix–Alajouanine syndrome is a progressive myelopathy produced by a spinal arteriovenous malformation. Patients are typically evaluated and the vascular lesion is identified by a neurologist, who directs spinal MRI and catheter angiography to establish the diagnosis; therefore, neurology is the medical specialty responsible for diagnosing the causative arteriovenous malformation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RAS-MAPK pathway",
    "long_answer": "Dominant mutations in the SOS1 gene—an activator of RAS—have been identified in families showing gingival fibromatosis accompanied by hypertrichosis. SOS1 lies upstream of RAF, MEK and ERK, so these mutations hyper-activate the RAS–MAPK signalling cascade. Hence the disorder is classified as a RASopathy, directly implicating the RAS-MAPK pathway."
}

{
    "short_answer": "COQ2",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function (absence) of the coenzyme-Q2 polyprenyltransferase gene COQ2, especially the East-Asian V393A variant and other null mutations, leads to reduced CoQ10 biosynthesis and has been shown (Science 2013) to occur far more frequently in Japanese and other Asian patients with multiple system atrophy than in controls, linking absence of functional COQ2 to the disease in these populations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cystic fibrosis",
    "long_answer": "Cystic fibrosis is a congenital (autosomal-recessive) disorder of the exocrine glands, including the salivary glands. The CFTR gene defect causes abnormally thick, viscous secretions; in the airways this impairs mucociliary clearance, leading to colonisation by pathogens and a markedly increased incidence of acute respiratory infections."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "BRCA1 (Breast Cancer 1) is a tumor-suppressor gene whose pathogenic variants are classically linked to hereditary breast and ovarian cancer syndromes, and, to a lesser extent, increased risks for other malignancies (e.g., prostate, pancreatic). BRCA1 is not part of the spectrum of genes known to cause disorders of keratinization such as palmoplantar keratoderma. Palmoplantar keratoderma is typically associated with mutations in genes encoding structural skin proteins (e.g., KRT1, KRT9, KRT6C, DSG1, LOR), enzymes such as cathepsin C (CTSC), or transcription factors like TP63, among others. No peer-reviewed studies, clinical genetics databases (OMIM, ClinVar, HGMD), or dermatology textbooks report an etiologic or mechanistic link between BRCA1 variants and palmoplantar keratoderma. Therefore, the statement that BRCA1 is associated with palmoplantar keratoderma is not supported by established biomedical evidence."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MAGI2",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the MAGI2 gene are a recognized cause of Infantile Epileptic Spasms Syndrome (IESS-7).  Independent reports have also shown that heterozygous loss-of-function or disruptive copy-number changes in MAGI2 lead to congenital hyperinsulinism, producing recurrent episodes of hypoglycemia.  Thus, the same gene that underlies infantile spasms can additionally manifest with hypoglycemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Serologic testing",
    "long_answer": "Bejel is caused by Treponema pallidum subsp. endemicum. The infection elicits circulating antibodies that are reliably detected with the same treponemal and non-treponemal serologic tests used for syphilis (e.g., FTA-ABS, TPPA, VDRL/RPR). Therefore, performing serologic testing allows identification of the antibodies produced against the pathogen and is the standard diagnostic method for bejel."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Basal ganglia",
    "long_answer": "In Neurofibromatosis type I, Unidentified Bright Objects (UBOs) appear as T2-hyperintense foci on MRI. They most frequently localize to the basal ganglia—particularly the globus pallidus and putamen—reflecting focal myelin vacuolization in this deep gray-matter region."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Alkaline phosphatase",
    "long_answer": "During an active flare-up of fibrodysplasia ossificans progressiva, new ectopic bone is laid down. This increased osteogenic activity commonly leads to a transient rise in the bone-derived enzyme alkaline phosphatase in the patient’s serum; therefore, elevated serum alkaline phosphatase is often used as a biochemical indicator that a flare-up is occurring."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Plasmapheresis",
    "long_answer": "In severe drug-induced skin reactions such as Stevens–Johnson syndrome or toxic epidermal necrolysis, therapeutic plasma exchange (plasmapheresis) is sometimes employed. The procedure removes circulating drug metabolites, immune complexes, and pro-inflammatory cytokines from the patient’s plasma, helping to halt epidermal destruction and improve outcomes when the reaction is life-threatening."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "The American Cancer Society (ACS) does not endorse therapeutic touch as an effective treatment for multiple chemical sensitivity (or for any other specific medical condition). In its reviews of complementary and alternative therapies, the ACS states that scientific evidence is lacking to show that therapeutic touch can prevent or cure disease, reduce symptoms, or improve health outcomes beyond a placebo effect. The organization emphasizes that well-controlled studies have not demonstrated objective benefits of therapeutic touch, and it advises patients to rely on proven, evidence-based treatments. Therefore, the claim that the ACS supports therapeutic touch for treating multiple chemical sensitivity is not consistent with the ACS’s published position."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Brain-lung-thyroid syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Brain-lung-thyroid syndrome is caused by pathogenic variants in the NKX2-1 (TTF-1) transcription factor. The disorder presents at birth with primary congenital hypothyroidism from thyroid dysgenesis, together with neonatal respiratory distress or chronic lung disease and variable neurological abnormalities. Impaired brain growth is frequent, so affected infants and children may develop microcephaly in addition to motor delay and chorea. Thus this single congenital syndrome links primary hypothyroidism with the potential for microcephaly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Concrescence",
    "long_answer": "Concrescence is a developmental dental anomaly in which two adjacent teeth become united solely by their cementum layers; the enamel and dentin remain separate. This fusion limited to cementum distinguishes concrescence from true tooth fusion, where dentin is also joined."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal chromosomes",
    "long_answer": "Rüdiger syndrome follows an autosomal-recessive pattern of inheritance; the causative mutations lie on one of the non-sex (autosomal) chromosomes, so the trait is transmitted independently of the sex chromosomes and affects males and females equally when both copies of the autosomal gene are defective."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MYH3 gene mutation",
    "long_answer": "Distal arthrogryposis is most commonly caused by autosomal-dominant missense mutations in the MYH3 gene, which encodes the embryonic skeletal-muscle myosin heavy chain. Altered myosin impairs fetal muscle contractility, leading to the characteristic distal joint contractures seen in this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pergolide or bromocriptine",
    "long_answer": "If low-dose levodopa does not fully control the dystonia in autosomal dominant GTP-cyclohydrolase I deficiency, dopamine agonists can be added. The agents most often recommended as this second-line option are the ergot-derived dopamine agonists pergolide and bromocriptine, which directly stimulate striatal D2/D1 receptors and can enhance dopaminergic tone when levodopa alone is insufficient or poorly tolerated."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Uncooked cornstarch",
    "long_answer": "In Glycogen Storage Disease type I, the cornerstone for preventing hypoglycemia is the scheduled ingestion of uncooked cornstarch, whose slow digestion supplies a steady stream of glucose. One level tablespoon of dry uncooked cornstarch (≈15 g) contains about 14 g of carbohydrate, providing roughly 55–60 kilocalories."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lutein",
    "long_answer": "Oral lutein supplementation is thought to help slow cone degeneration in hereditary cone or cone-rod dystrophies. Lutein is a macular carotenoid that concentrates in the fovea, where it scavenges reactive oxygen species and absorbs blue light, thereby protecting cone photoreceptors. Small clinical studies have shown that daily lutein increases macular pigment optical density and is associated with a slower decline in cone-mediated visual function, supporting its use to delay progression of cone dystrophy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Surgical cyst fenestration",
    "long_answer": "When an arachnoid cyst produces neurological symptoms, one accepted therapeutic option is surgical cyst fenestration—either microsurgical or endoscopic. Opening (fenestrating) the cyst wall allows its cerebrospinal fluid to communicate with normal CSF spaces, relieving mass effect and lowering intracystic pressure, which typically improves or resolves symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurology",
    "long_answer": "Hyperreflexia is a classic upper-motor-neuron sign that follows damage to descending inhibitory pathways in the spinal cord. Evaluation and management of spinal cord injuries and their neurological sequelae, including exaggerated reflexes, fall under the domain of neurology, the medical specialty focused on disorders of the central and peripheral nervous system."
}

{
    "short_answer": "British Guiana",
    "long_answer": "Although ainhum was originally recognised in West Africa, the first report of the condition from the Western hemisphere came from the then-British colony of British Guiana (present-day Guyana), making it the earliest British colonial site in the West to document the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "KCNT1",
    "long_answer": "De novo gain-of-function mutations in the KCNT1 gene, which encodes the sodium-activated potassium channel KNa1.1, are the most common genetic cause of malignant migrating partial seizures of infancy (MMPSI). Patients with KCNT1-related MMPSI often develop progressive cerebral and cerebellar atrophy on neuroimaging, leading to enlarged ventricles and widened subarachnoid spaces—i.e., marked changes in the cerebrospinal fluid spaces."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cor pulmonale",
    "long_answer": "Chronic mountain sickness (Monge’s disease) causes sustained hypoxemia that drives excessive erythrocytosis and marked pulmonary vasoconstriction. The resulting pulmonary hypertension places an increasing pressure load on the right ventricle; if the process is not corrected, right-ventricular hypertrophy and dilation ensue, culminating in cor pulmonale (right-sided heart failure). Thus, untreated chronic mountain sickness can progress to cor pulmonale."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cytomegalovirus retinitis",
    "long_answer": "Cytomegalovirus (CMV) retinitis is an opportunistic infection that occurs almost exclusively in people with severely compromised immune systems, such as those with advanced HIV/AIDS or after organ transplantation. The disease primarily affects the retina, producing visual disturbances (floaters, blurry vision, scotomata) rather than systemic illness. Although low-grade fever can occasionally be reported, it is an uncommon finding; most patients have no fever or other systemic symptoms, making fever a rare presentation in CMV retinitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "de novo gene birth",
    "long_answer": "New protein-coding genes can arise directly from previously non-coding DNA through de novo gene birth (also called de novo gene origination). During this process, random mutations create an open reading frame in a non-coding region, followed by acquisition of regulatory elements that allow transcription and translation. Natural selection can then fix functional variants, yielding a novel gene that, in some cases—such as loci implicated in Noonan syndrome—may influence disease phenotypes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Down syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Down syndrome (trisomy 21) is a chromosomal disorder in which newborns typically show generalized hypotonia, resulting in weak or absent primitive neonatal reflexes such as the Moro and grasp reflexes. At birth they also display characteristic dysmorphic facial features—flat facial profile, up-slanting palpebral fissures, epicanthal folds, small ears, and a flat nasal bridge. The combination of absent neonatal reflexes and abnormal facial appearance is therefore classically associated with Down syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Kaiser Wilhelm II",
    "long_answer": "German Emperor Kaiser Wilhelm II (1859-1941) suffered a traction injury to the brachial plexus during a difficult breech delivery, damaging the upper roots (C5–C6) and producing the characteristic arm weakness and contracture of Erb’s palsy that he displayed throughout his life."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Intestinal malrotation",
    "long_answer": "In Noonan syndrome, developmental anomalies of the mid-gut can occur, most notably intestinal malrotation. When the intestines fail to rotate and fix properly during embryogenesis, narrow mesenteric attachments leave the mid-gut prone to twisting around the superior mesenteric vessels, producing a mid-gut (intestinal) volvulus. Thus, intestinal malrotation is the congenital anomaly linked to Noonan syndrome that can precipitate volvulus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Kaiser Wilhelm II",
    "long_answer": "Kaiser Wilhelm II of Germany was born in 1859 with a traumatic obstetric injury to the upper brachial plexus (C5–C6 roots), producing Erb-Duchenne palsy. The resulting paralysis and contracture left his left arm shortened and weakened, a well-documented disability throughout his life."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ole Lyngstadaas",
    "long_answer": "The condition carries the name of its discoverer, the Norwegian physician Ole Lyngstadaas, who first described the autosomal-recessive cholestatic liver disease that is now referred to as Lyngstadaas syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cri du chat syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Cri du chat syndrome, caused by a deletion of the short arm of chromosome 5 (5p-), presents with marked intellectual disability, a characteristic dysmorphic facial appearance (hypertelorism, epicanthal folds, micrognathia, round face) and is classically accompanied by microcephaly. These features make it the well-recognized genetic disorder linking distinctive facies, cognitive impairment and microcephaly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Classical conditioning",
    "long_answer": "Environmental or internal cues repeatedly paired with alcohol’s rewarding pharmacological effects become conditioned stimuli through classical (Pavlovian) conditioning. These conditioned stimuli later evoke craving and drinking automatically, thus perpetuating and maintaining alcohol dependence even in the absence of the original reinforcing effects."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Social anxiety disorder",
    "long_answer": "Ergophobia (an excessive fear of work or the workplace) is typically classified as a situational or performance-related manifestation of social anxiety disorder (social phobia). Large epidemiologic studies and meta-analyses show that people with social anxiety disorder have significantly higher rates of suicidal ideation and suicide attempts than the general population, giving this disorder a recognized high risk of suicide."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Liver biopsy enzyme assay",
    "long_answer": "Confirmation of Glycogen Storage Disease type I is obtained by performing a liver biopsy and directly measuring glucose-6-phosphatase activity in the tissue; absent or markedly reduced enzymatic activity establishes the diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hematuria",
    "long_answer": "In distal (type I) renal tubular acidosis, the persistently alkaline urine promotes calcium-phosphate stone formation and nephrocalcinosis. The resulting stones or crystal deposits often irritate the urinary tract, leading to microscopic or gross blood in the urine. Therefore, hematuria—defined as the presence of blood in the urine—is a common clinical symptom associated with distal renal tubular acidosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurology",
    "long_answer": "X-linked myopathy with excessive autophagy is a rare inherited neuromuscular disorder that presents with progressive muscle weakness. The evaluation and diagnosis of such primary muscle diseases—including clinical examination, electromyography, muscle biopsy interpretation, and ordering of confirmatory genetic testing—are typically performed by neurologists, especially those with neuromuscular subspecialty training. Therefore, the medical specialty most likely to establish the diagnosis is Neurology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Elliptical trainer",
    "long_answer": "An elliptical trainer (also called a cross-trainer) guides the feet through an oval, or elliptical, path that merges the vertical stepping motion of a stair-climber with the horizontal stride of walking or running on a treadmill. This hybrid movement allows users to obtain the cardiovascular benefits of both exercises while minimizing joint impact."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Developmental and epileptic encephalopathy",
    "long_answer": "In the ILAE 2022 classification of the epilepsies, Dravet syndrome is listed under the group of “developmental and epileptic encephalopathies” (DEE). These syndromes are characterized by early-onset, treatment-resistant seizures accompanied by developmental slowing or regression that is partly driven by the epileptic activity itself. Dravet syndrome, typically linked to pathogenic SCN1A variants, fits this definition and is explicitly named as a prototypical DEE in the 2022 ILAE framework."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Spondylocostal dysostosis",
    "long_answer": "Spondylocostal dysostosis (Jarcho-Levin syndrome) causes multiple segmentation defects of the vertebrae and ribs. Prenatal ultrasound often shows ribs of unequal length and irregular spacing that can mimic the multiple rib fractures seen in lethal osteogenesis imperfecta type II, leading to possible misdiagnosis. Recognizing the asymmetric, congenital pattern of rib malformations—rather than true fractures—helps differentiate spondylocostal dysostosis from osteogenesis imperfecta." 
}

{
    "short_answer": "Doxycycline",
    "long_answer": "Doxycycline, a long-acting member of the tetracycline class, is given as 200 mg orally once weekly and has been shown in controlled trials to prevent leptospiral infection in people exposed to contaminated water or soil. Beyond its medical role, doxycycline is frequently embedded in or blended with molten polycarbonate during moulding of medical-grade plastic devices; the antibiotic remains in the polymer matrix and is slowly released, giving the finished polycarbonate material intrinsic antimicrobial properties."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Plain X-ray radiography",
    "long_answer": "Anteroposterior and lateral plain X-ray films of the cervical spine and shoulder girdle show the elevated, medially rotated scapula (and any omovertebral bone), thereby confirming Sprengel’s deformity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "U.S. Army",
    "long_answer": "Epidemiologic studies of post-9/11 service members show that soldiers in the U.S. Army, the branch most frequently deployed in prolonged ground-combat operations, exhibit the highest rates of post-traumatic stress disorder compared with other branches (e.g., Navy, Air Force). Intensive, repeated combat exposure among Army personnel is the primary driver of this elevated PTSD prevalence."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Alport syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Da Costa’s (effort) syndrome was studied and described in First-World-War soldiers by the British physician Arthur Cecil Alport. The same clinician later (1927) reported a familial form of progressive hereditary nephritis with deafness, which has since been named Alport syndrome. Thus, the genetic disorder associated with the physician who described Da Costa’s syndrome is Alport syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PAX2",
    "long_answer": "The PAX2 gene encodes a transcription factor that is essential for normal ureteric-bud outgrowth and branching during kidney development. Abnormal expression or mutation of PAX2 disrupts the signalling between the ureteric bud and the metanephric mesenchyme, leading to renal dysplasia with cyst formation. Elevated or altered PAX2 activity has been repeatedly documented in human multicystic dysplastic kidney specimens, and inherited PAX2 mutations produce syndromes (e.g., renal-coloboma) in which multicystic dysplastic kidneys are a characteristic finding. Hence PAX2 is the gene most commonly implicated in the pathogenesis of multicystic dysplastic kidney."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TGM5",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in TGM5, the gene that encodes epidermal transglutaminase-5, cause acral/peeling skin syndrome. Affected individuals typically show lifelong superficial peeling of the stratum corneum, especially on hands and feet, and many patients also exhibit palmoplantar hyperhidrosis. Thus TGM5 is the gene linked to both peeling skin syndrome and excessive sweating (hyperhidrosis)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hearing aids",
    "long_answer": "Saal-Bulas syndrome (a SALL4-related Duane–radial-ray disorder) commonly includes congenital conductive or sensorineural hearing loss. The standard management of this particular symptom is early audiologic assessment and fitting of hearing aids, which restore auditory input, support speech-language development, and improve quality of life."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chills",
    "long_answer": "Erysipelas often presents with abrupt high fever accompanied by chills— involuntary shivering episodes that raise body temperature. Chills are the same physiological response the body mounts when exposed to cold or during extreme fear, mediated by hypothalamic thermoregulatory centers and sympathetic activation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Banana",
    "long_answer": "Paramyotonia congenita is a sodium-channel myopathy in which attacks are precipitated by cold, exercise, or an acute rise in serum potassium. Eating potassium-rich foods, classically bananas, can raise serum K⁺ and thereby trigger the characteristic stiffness and weakness in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Maternal anti-HBs IgG",
    "long_answer": "IgG antibodies directed against hepatitis B surface antigen (anti-HBs) are actively transported across the placenta via Fc receptors. These maternal IgG molecules coat and neutralise any hepatitis B virions that may reach the intervillous space, so the fetus is passively protected and in-utero transmission is largely prevented; most neonatal infection therefore occurs during labour and delivery rather than across the intact placenta."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tibialis anterior",
    "long_answer": "In the autosomal-dominant pure form of hereditary spastic paraplegia, weakness is most prominent in ankle dorsiflexion; the tibialis anterior muscle, the principal dorsiflexor of the foot, is characteristically weak, leading to early foot-drop despite otherwise predominant spasticity of the lower limbs."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Patent ductus arteriosus",
    "long_answer": "In published case series of females with tetrasomy X (48,XXXX), congenital heart disease is relatively common; the lesion reported most often is persistence of the fetal ductus arteriosus. Patent ductus arteriosus has been documented more frequently than other defects such as atrial or ventricular septal defects, making it the heart defect most commonly associated with tetrasomy X."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TEK",
    "long_answer": "Dr. Janey Wiggs’ group showed that loss-of-function mutations in the TEK gene (also called TIE2), which encodes an endothelial receptor tyrosine kinase essential for Schlemm’s canal formation, cause primary congenital glaucoma by impairing aqueous humor outflow and raising intra-ocular pressure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Malnutrition",
    "long_answer": "When dysphagia is not managed, patients often cannot take in enough food or fluids, leading to protein-energy and micronutrient deficits—i.e., malnutrition. Chronic or severe gastritis can likewise reduce appetite, cause vomiting, impair nutrient absorption, and produce chronic blood loss, all of which are well-documented pathways to the same condition. Thus malnutrition is both a consequence of untreated dysphagia and a recognized complication of gastritis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Oculoplastic surgeon",
    "long_answer": "A trichofolliculoma on the eyelid is an eyelid skin adnexal lesion whose excision and reconstruction must preserve eyelid margin integrity and ocular surface protection. These procedures fall within the subspecialty of oculoplastic (ophthalmic plastic and reconstructive) surgery, a branch of ophthalmology trained to manage benign and malignant eyelid tumors safely while maintaining eyelid function and cosmesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lumbrical muscle",
    "long_answer": "In Filippi syndrome the cutaneous syndactyly of the fingers frequently extends to the deeper structures; the lumbrical muscle can span the fused digits and limit independent movement. Corrective hand surgery therefore often includes division or excision of this aberrant lumbrical muscle to allow proper separation and function of the affected fingers."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hematology",
    "long_answer": "Leukemias, including the minimally differentiated (M0) subtype of acute myeloblastic (myeloid) leukemia, are managed by hematologists (often trained as hematologist-oncologists). This specialty focuses on disorders of the blood and bone marrow and directs diagnostic evaluation, chemotherapy, targeted therapy, and stem-cell transplantation for AML patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hyponatremia",
    "long_answer": "In patients with schizophrenia who develop psychogenic polydipsia, dilutional hyponatremia commonly accompanies the excessive water intake. The large volumes of free water ingested overwhelm renal excretory capacity, lowering serum sodium and leading to water-intoxication episodes; therefore, hyponatremia is considered a frequent comorbid condition with psychogenic polydipsia in this population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Distal phalanges",
    "long_answer": "Biemond syndrome (also called the coloboma–brachydactyly syndrome) is classically associated with brachydactyly that involves the terminal, or distal, phalanges of the fingers and toes. These end-bones are shortened or absent, whereas the more proximal hand and foot bones are usually normal."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Retina",
    "long_answer": "The visual problems in Cohen syndrome arise from a progressive pigmentary retinopathy—degeneration of the retinal tissue, which is an outgrowth of the brain. Damage to the retina leads to high myopia, night blindness, and progressive loss of visual acuity seen in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "≈7.4",
    "long_answer": "Escherichia coli grows best, and is able to initiate infection, in a neutral-to-slightly alkaline milieu. The peritoneal cavity is normally close to physiologic pH (about 7.35–7.45). If E. coli escapes the intestine into this environment, the neutral pH (~7.4) permits rapid bacterial multiplication and the release of endotoxin, leading to acute bacterial peritonitis. In more acidic conditions (pH < 6), its growth is markedly suppressed, but at the physiologic pH of ~7.4 it can readily cause peritoneal infection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Enteropathic arthritis",
    "long_answer": "Hypersensitivity to wheat gluten, as in coeliac (gluten-sensitive) disease, is well documented to cause a seronegative spondyloarthropathy termed enteropathic arthritis. This arthritis is regarded as an extra-intestinal manifestation of gluten/wheat sensitivity and typically involves large peripheral joints and sometimes the sacro-iliac joints."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PIKFYVE",
    "long_answer": "Corneal-cerebellar syndrome combines bilateral fleck-type corneal dystrophy with progressive cerebellar ataxia. Families with this phenotype have been shown to carry heterozygous missense mutations in the PIKFYVE gene, which encodes phosphoinositide kinase FYVE-type zinc finger–containing enzyme. These mutations disrupt phosphoinositide metabolism in both corneal keratocytes and cerebellar neurons, accounting for the characteristic corneal dystrophy and neurologic involvement seen in the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cervical trachea",
    "long_answer": "A short Type IV laryngeal cleft reaches from the larynx into the upper (cervical) segment of the trachea. Surgical repair is done through a lateral cervical (neck) approach that exposes and closes the cleft in the posterior wall of the cervical trachea."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pierre Joseph Bonnaterre",
    "long_answer": "The Martinican pit viper, now classified as Bothrops lanceolatus, was first formally described in 1790 by the French zoologist Pierre Joseph Bonnaterre, whose name is cited as the taxonomic authority for the species."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Immunosuppressive drugs",
    "long_answer": "The nephropathy in multicentric carpotarsal osteolysis syndrome is a non-immune, MAFB-related focal segmental glomerulosclerosis. Multiple case reports show that corticosteroids, cyclosporine and other immunosuppressive agents do not halt proteinuria or prevent progression to renal failure, indicating that immunosuppressive drugs are ineffective for this renal complication."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IRF6",
    "long_answer": "Genome‐wide association studies have consistently identified variants in the IRF6 gene (Interferon Regulatory Factor 6) as strongly associated with cleft lip and/or palate. IRF6 mutations underlie Van der Woude syndrome, the most common syndromic form of craniofacial clefting, and common non-coding variants at the IRF6 locus are a major genetic risk factor for nonsyndromic cleft lip with or without cleft palate."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ischemic (hypoxic) cell injury",
    "long_answer": "The cutaneous reaction of livedoid dermatitis (Nicolau syndrome) after drug injections is produced by acute ischemia of the skin and subcutaneous tissues, caused by arterial spasm or thrombotic occlusion at the injection site. This ischemia deprives cells of oxygen, leading to the characteristic ischemic (hypoxic) necrosis that underlies the lesion."
}

{
    "short_answer": "APP gene mutation",
    "long_answer": "A missense or duplicative mutation in the amyloid precursor protein (APP) gene, located on chromosome 21, is a well-established cause of autosomal-dominant early-onset Alzheimer’s disease. The altered APP undergoes aberrant β- and γ-secretase cleavage, generating excess Aβ42 peptides that aggregate into extracellular amyloid plaques. These amyloid deposits trigger tau hyper-phosphorylation inside neurons, resulting in the intracellular neurofibrillary tangles that are pathognomonic of Alzheimer’s disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fusobacterium nucleatum",
    "long_answer": "Fusobacterium nucleatum, an oral anaerobic Gram-negative bacterium, is consistently enriched in colorectal cancer tissue and stool. Its abundance rises from normal mucosa to adenomas and carcinomas, and it promotes tumorigenesis through factors such as the FadA adhesin and immune modulation. Quantitative detection of F. nucleatum DNA in fecal samples accurately discriminates patients with advanced adenomas or early colorectal cancer, making it a useful biomarker for early prediction of high-risk bowel lesions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Activated charcoal",
    "long_answer": "Activated charcoal is routinely administered in cases of barbiturate overdose because it adsorbs the drug in the gastrointestinal tract and limits systemic absorption. The same gastrointestinal decontamination approach is widely employed for many other acute drug overdoses (e.g., tricyclic antidepressants, acetaminophen), making activated charcoal a common, broad-spectrum initial treatment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Congenital toxoplasmosis",
    "long_answer": "If a woman acquires primary Toxoplasma gondii infection during pregnancy, the parasite can cross the placenta and infect the fetus, leading to congenital toxoplasmosis. This can result in miscarriage, stillbirth, or severe neonatal complications such as hydrocephalus, chorioretinitis, and intracranial calcifications."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GABAA receptor",
    "long_answer": "Neuropathological studies of Unverricht–Lundborg disease (EPM1) brains show an up-regulation of the γ-aminobutyric acid type A (GABAA) receptor. The density of these inhibitory neurotransmitter receptors is significantly higher than in unaffected brains, a finding that correlates with the clinical responsiveness of patients to benzodiazepines, which act by potentiating GABAA receptor–mediated inhibition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Blalock-Taussig shunt",
    "long_answer": "In infants with tricuspid atresia, a systemic-to-pulmonary arterial shunt such as the Blalock-Taussig shunt is commonly created to augment pulmonary blood flow. By connecting the subclavian (or innominate) artery to the pulmonary artery, this palliative procedure directs oxygen-poor systemic blood into the lungs, improving oxygenation until later definitive surgeries (e.g., Glenn or Fontan) can be performed."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ketamine",
    "long_answer": "In the novel, mission director Eva Stratt has Ryland Grace injected with ketamine, a dissociative anesthetic that produces profound sedation and both retrograde and anterograde amnesia. The drug’s well-known ability to erase recent memories and render a patient unconscious ensured Grace would not recall being forced onto the Hail Mary spacecraft."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Paresthesia",
    "long_answer": "Beyond progressive lower-limb spasticity, many individuals with hereditary spastic paraplegia report paresthesia—abnormal skin sensations such as tingling, prickling, or ‘pins-and-needles.’ These sensory disturbances reflect involvement of long sensory tracts alongside corticospinal tract degeneration characteristic of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Nager syndrome (acrofacial dysostosis, Nager type) is most often produced by heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the SF3B4 gene on chromosome 1. Because the defective allele resides on an autosome and a single copy is sufficient to cause the condition, the disorder follows an autosomal dominant Mendelian inheritance pattern."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X-linked recessive",
    "long_answer": "Familial benign copper deficiency results from mutations in the ATP7A copper-transport gene located on the X chromosome. Because the mutant allele is carried on the X chromosome and disease manifests primarily in hemizygous males while heterozygous females are usually asymptomatic carriers, the condition follows an X-linked recessive mode of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mitochondria",
    "long_answer": "Glycine encephalopathy (non-ketotic hyperglycinemia) is caused by defects in the mitochondrial glycine cleavage system. Because this multi-enzyme complex resides on the inner mitochondrial membrane, mitochondrial dysfunction in glycine degradation leads to systemic accumulation of glycine and the characteristic neurological symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Polymerase chain reaction",
    "long_answer": "In minimally differentiated (M0) acute myeloblastic leukemia, cytochemical stains for myeloperoxidase are usually negative, so lineage must be confirmed molecularly. This is done by amplifying and detecting sequences of the myeloperoxidase (MPO) gene with the polymerase chain reaction (PCR), demonstrating the presence of MPO despite absent enzyme activity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pee Dee River",
    "long_answer": "In the early-20th-century United States, the most notorious pellagra epidemics were concentrated in the impoverished mill towns and farm communities of the Pee Dee River basin in South and North Carolina. Public-health investigators, including Joseph Goldberger, documented thousands of cases along this river, and the region became emblematic of the U.S. pellagra outbreak linked to monotonous corn-based diets deficient in niacin."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Baclofen",
    "long_answer": "Spasticity in Megalencephalic leukoencephalopathy with subcortical cysts (MLC) is managed symptomatically, and the antispastic agent most frequently prescribed is baclofen. Baclofen, a GABA-B receptor agonist, reduces excitatory neurotransmitter release in the spinal cord and thereby diminishes muscle hypertonia, making it the standard first-line pharmacologic therapy for spasticity in MLC and other leukodystrophies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Variable expressivity",
    "long_answer": "Among people who carry the same causal genetic alteration for Müllerian agenesis (MRKH syndrome), the trait does not manifest to the same degree in every individual. This phenomenon, known as variable expressivity, means that identical or similar mutations can produce a spectrum of clinical features—from complete absence of the uterus and upper vagina to milder malformations—thereby explaining the differing severity of symptoms observed in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Paraganglia / paraganglioma",
    "long_answer": "Carney-Stratakis syndrome (the ʻdyadʼ) is defined by the coexistence of gastrointestinal stromal tumours (GISTs) with catecholamine-secreting paragangliomas. These tumours arise from the neuroendocrine paraganglia (chromaffin tissue related to the adrenal medulla) and may function like pheochromocytomas by producing catecholamines; thus the hormone-producing glandular element involved is the paraganglionic system."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Benzodiazepines",
    "long_answer": "Movement disorders such as dystonia and choreoathetosis that develop after kernicterus are often treated with benzodiazepines (e.g., clonazepam or diazepam). These drugs enhance GABA-mediated inhibition within the basal ganglia and other motor pathways, helping to dampen the hyperkinetic movements produced by bilirubin-induced neuronal injury."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Molybdenum deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Molybdenum is an essential cofactor for xanthine oxidase, the enzyme that converts xanthine to uric acid. When dietary molybdenum is insufficient, xanthine oxidase activity falls, xanthine accumulates, and poorly soluble xanthine precipitates in the renal tract, leading to kidney (xanthine) stones."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ATXN7",
    "long_answer": "Autosomal dominant cerebellar ataxia type II corresponds to spinocerebellar ataxia 7, which is caused by CAG-repeat expansion in the ATXN7 gene (ataxin-7). Ataxin-7 is an integral subunit of the SAGA (Spt–Ada–GCN5 acetyltransferase) transcription co-activator complex, and its transcription is regulated through the chromatin-remodeling and histone-acetylation activities of SAGA. Hence, ATXN7 is the gene whose expression is controlled by the SAGA complex."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Yes. In the United States, the National Institutes of Health (NIH)—specifically its Office of Rare Diseases Research (ORDR) within the National Center for Advancing Translational Sciences (NCATS)—maintains the Genetic and Rare Diseases Information Center (GARD). GARD includes an entry for “Fetal trimethadione syndrome” (also called “trimethadione embryopathy”) and designates it as a rare disease, i.e., affecting fewer than 200,000 people in the U.S. Therefore, at least one government agency (the NIH) officially classifies fetal trimethadione syndrome as a rare disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "COL4A1 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the COL4A1 gene (encoding the α1-chain of type IV collagen) are a well-documented cause of hereditary cerebral small-vessel disease that often presents prenatally as intracerebral haemorrhage and porencephaly. Several families and case reports have also shown that children with COL4A1 mutations display the characteristic facial gestalt and neuro-behavioural profile used to diagnose fetal alcohol spectrum disorders, leading to recognition of COL4A1 mutation as a genetic mimic and risk factor for FASD as well as a proven cause of porencephaly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sarcoidosis",
    "long_answer": "Several New York City firefighters who inhaled the caustic, silica- and metal-laden dust cloud on 9/11 subsequently developed sarcoidosis—a multisystem granulomatous disease characterized by non-caseating granulomas, most often in the lungs and lymph nodes. Epidemiologic studies of FDNY personnel showed a markedly increased incidence of new-onset sarcoidosis after World Trade Center dust exposure, linking the inhaled particulate matter to the firefighter’s disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Idiopathic onycholysis",
    "long_answer": "Onycholysis is the distal separation of the nail plate from the nail bed. When a thorough evaluation fails to reveal any local trauma, dermatologic disorder, systemic illness, infection, or drug exposure responsible for the detachment, the condition is classified as idiopathic (or primary) onycholysis, meaning the cause is unknown."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TikTok",
    "long_answer": "The short-form video platform TikTok propelled the album “Everywhere at the End of Time” by The Caretaker to viral status; users shared clips and reactions that simulated progressive memory loss, sparking widespread discussion and raising public awareness about the clinical features of dementia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SYNE1",
    "long_answer": "The autosomal-recessive pure cerebellar ataxia that Mary Harding delineated (sometimes termed ARCA1 or “Harding ataxia”) was later genetically mapped to chromosome 6 and shown to be caused by biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the SYNE1 gene, which encodes the nuclear-envelope protein nesprin-1. Hence, SYNE1 is the gene associated with the ataxia described by Harding."
}

{
    "short_answer": "1 in 5,000 live births",
    "long_answer": "The congenital malformation that affects the anus—commonly termed imperforate anus or anorectal malformation—occurs in roughly one out of every 5,000 live births. This figure is consistently reported in large epidemiologic studies and pediatric surgery textbooks, reflecting the accepted incidence rate for this congenital disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Left ventricular aneurysmectomy",
    "long_answer": "Left ventricular aneurysmectomy is the standard operation for a post-infarction ventricular aneurysm. During the procedure the thinned, scarred aneurysmal portion of the ventricular wall is excised and the ventricle is closed (often with a patch), thereby reducing the diameter and volume of the ventricle, restoring a more normal geometry, and improving systolic function."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Heart failure",
    "long_answer": "Hashimoto’s thyroiditis often progresses to chronic hypothyroidism, which slows cardiac contractility, increases systemic vascular resistance, and promotes atherogenic lipid profiles; over time these changes can enlarge the heart and precipitate congestive heart failure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Type III collagen",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in type III collagen (encoded by COL3A1) underlie the vascular form of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome, a connective-tissue disorder. Because type III collagen is a critical structural component of arterial walls, its deficiency weakens cerebral arteries and is well documented to predispose patients to the formation and rupture of intracranial aneurysms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SDHB",
    "long_answer": "Germ-line mutations in the succinate dehydrogenase subunit B (SDHB) gene define hereditary paraganglioma syndrome type 4. This syndrome typically presents with abdominal paragangliomas (historically called abdominal chemodectomas) and is characteristically accompanied by multiple cutaneous angiolipomas. Loss-of-function of SDHB, a component of mitochondrial complex II, leads to succinate accumulation and pseudohypoxic signaling that drives tumorigenesis, explaining the link between SDHB mutation and the combined occurrence of abdominal chemodectomas with cutaneous angiolipomas."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HSD11B2 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the HSD11B2 gene, which encodes 11β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 2, prevent the conversion of active cortisol to inactive cortisone. The resulting prolonged tissue exposure to glucocorticoids (‘apparent mineralocorticoid excess’) leads to severe neonatal hypertension, hypokalaemia and failure to thrive, a rare but potentially fatal complication that can present within the first year of life."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SURF1",
    "long_answer": "Genetic testing of Joseph Maraachli showed a pathogenic mutation in the SURF1 gene. SURF1 encodes a protein required for proper assembly of cytochrome-c oxidase (complex IV) in the mitochondrial respiratory chain; loss-of-function mutations in SURF1 are a well-established cause of classic, early-onset Leigh syndrome, explaining the progressive neurodegeneration observed in the infant."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Psychiatry",
    "long_answer": "PHACE syndrome can be associated with developmental, behavioral, and emotional problems such as attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder, anxiety, or mood disturbances. Management of these mental health conditions is handled by the medical specialty of Psychiatry, which provides evaluation, pharmacologic therapy, and coordination of psychological support for affected patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cor pulmonale",
    "long_answer": "If Central hypoventilation syndrome is not treated, chronic nocturnal hypoventilation leads to persistent hypoxemia and hypercapnia. The sustained hypoxemia causes pulmonary vasoconstriction, progressing to pulmonary hypertension. Over time, the right ventricle must pump against this elevated pressure, resulting in right-sided heart enlargement and failure—clinically termed cor pulmonale."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Philadelphia chromosome",
    "long_answer": "The Philadelphia chromosome, a reciprocal translocation t(9;22)(q34;q11) that fuses the BCR gene on chromosome 22 to ABL1 on chromosome 9, is pathognomonic for chronic myelogenous leukemia and is also detected in a subset of acute myeloid leukemia cases (\"Ph-positive AML\"). The resulting BCR-ABL tyrosine-kinase drives leukemogenesis in both diseases, making this chromosomal abnormality common to AML and CML."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Covering water tanks",
    "long_answer": "Aedes mosquitoes that transmit dengue breed in stagnant clean water stored in open containers. Placing tight-fitting lids or screens over household water tanks prevents the insects from accessing the water to lay eggs, thereby interrupting their life-cycle and lowering dengue transmission risk."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tumor necrosis factor-α (TNF-α)",
    "long_answer": "TNF-α is a major pro-inflammatory cytokine released in response to Shiga toxin and lipopolysaccharide during infections that precipitate Hemolytic–uremic syndrome. Elevated TNF-α levels up-regulate the Shiga-toxin receptor (Gb3) on endothelial cells, amplify complement and coagulation activation, and drive the endothelial injury and microvascular thrombosis characteristic of HUS, making it a key cytokine implicated in the syndrome’s pathogenesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Risperidone",
    "long_answer": "Case reports and small series of patients with Kleine–Levin syndrome (KLS) show that the atypical antipsychotic risperidone can diminish hallucinations, paranoia, and other psychotic-like behaviours that sometimes accompany hypersomnic episodes. By antagonising dopamine D2 and serotonin 5-HT2 receptors, risperidone helps stabilise dopaminergic-serotonergic imbalance thought to underlie these transient psychotic symptoms, making it a useful option when such manifestations are prominent in KLS."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Clubfoot",
    "long_answer": "In Sharon E. McKay’s novel, Charlie Wilcox is born with congenital clubfoot (talipes equinovarus). This orthopaedic deformity twists the feet inward and downward, often causing pain, limping, or difficulty walking. Believing the condition would keep Charlie from tolerating the rigors of fishing or military service, his parents assume he is unfit for an active, physically demanding life."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Myoclonus",
    "long_answer": "Sialidosis type I is classically known as the cherry-red spot-myoclonus syndrome, with stimulus-sensitive myoclonic jerks being one of its hallmark features. Myoclonus—sudden, brief, involuntary muscle contractions—also occurs in several other neurological disorders, including Parkinson’s disease, where cortical or subcortical myoclonus can appear, especially in advanced stages or as a treatment complication. Thus, myoclonus is a shared symptom between sialidosis and neurodegenerative diseases such as Parkinson’s."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Isla de Margarita",
    "long_answer": "Population genetic studies have identified a founder effect on Isla de Margarita (Margarita Island) off the Venezuelan coast. Because of this isolated population history, the carrier frequency of the autosomal-dominant Rosselli–Gulienetti ectodermal dysplasia syndrome is markedly elevated among island inhabitants compared with other regions of Venezuela."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vulva",
    "long_answer": "Genital warts, produced by infection with low-risk human papillomavirus (especially HPV-6 and HPV-11), typically arise on the moist epithelial surfaces of the female external genitalia, most characteristically the vulva (including the labia). They may also be found on the cervix, vagina or perianal skin, but the vulva is a common and well-recognized site."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Reduced self-efficacy",
    "long_answer": "Experiencing weight-related stigma promotes internalized shame and stress, which undermines individuals’ confidence in their ability to control eating, exercise, and weight-management behaviors. Studies show that exposure to stigmatizing messages or discrimination leads to lower ratings on self-efficacy scales and poorer adherence to health behaviors, demonstrating that obesity stigma directly reduces self-efficacy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Transmission electron microscopy",
    "long_answer": "Thin-section transmission electron microscopy was employed to visualize and chronologically map capsid assembly, nuclear egress, envelopment, and virion release, allowing investigators to define the successive stages of the lytic infection cycle of Herpes simplex virus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prolactin",
    "long_answer": "Bangstad syndrome (also called hypoparathyroidism-retardation-dysmorphism or Sanjad-Sakati syndrome) is marked by congenital hypoparathyroidism with severe hypocalcaemia. A consistent endocrine finding in affected patients is persistent hyperprolactinaemia, with serum prolactin concentrations well above the normal range. This elevation of prolactin accompanies the hypoparathyroidism that characterises the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vulva",
    "long_answer": "Benign eccrine-duct tumours (syringomas) can develop on the vulvar skin, and several case reports document vulvar syringomas occurring in patients who have Hailey–Hailey disease, a genodermatosis that commonly involves intertriginous and genital areas. This anatomic association is well described in the dermatologic literature."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Thoracic vertebrae",
    "long_answer": "Transverse myelitis most frequently involves the thoracic segment of the spinal cord, especially the mid-thoracic (T4–T8) levels. This region is a vascular watershed zone with relatively limited collateral blood supply, making it more susceptible to inflammatory demyelination and ischemia that characterize transverse myelitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "-12 °C",
    "long_answer": "Soft contact lenses are made from the hydrogel obtained by polymerising 2-hydroxyethyl methacrylate (2-HEMA). The purified 2-HEMA monomer is a colourless liquid that solidifies at about –12 °C; this value is its melting (freezing) point and is a standard physical constant reported for the compound."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ligation",
    "long_answer": "In the surgical repair of an interrupted aortic arch, the surgeon often performs ligation—physically tying off (and usually dividing) the patent ductus arteriosus or other aberrant vessels. Ligation stops aberrant flow and allows reconstruction of the aortic arch, making this vessel-tying manoeuvre a key technique in treating the defect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cavernous hemangioma",
    "long_answer": "Large cavernous hemangiomas can trap and sequester platelets within their wide, blood-filled vascular spaces. This platelet sequestration typically produces only a mild thrombocytopenia, unlike the severe consumptive coagulopathy seen with kaposiform hemangioendothelioma or tufted angioma. Thus, cavernous hemangioma is the vascular tumor characteristically associated with a mild drop in platelet count."
}

{
    "short_answer": "10q24 duplication",
    "long_answer": "Ectrodactyly-polydactyly syndrome (split hand/foot malformation) is most commonly produced by a recurrent tandem duplication of the chromosome 10q24 region (the SHFM3 locus). The extra copy of this segment, which contains genes such as FBXW4 (\"dactylin\"), disrupts normal apical ectodermal ridge signalling during limb development, leading to the characteristic congenital malformations of ectrodactyly and polydactyly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prednisone",
    "long_answer": "Prednisone, a systemic glucocorticoid, is a first-line drug for immune thrombocytopenic purpura because it suppresses auto-antibody formation and promotes platelet recovery. The same broad immunosuppressive effect makes prednisone a routine component of maintenance therapy after solid-organ transplantation, where it is given with other agents to prevent graft rejection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Taurine",
    "long_answer": "Taurine is a sulfur-containing amino acid that reaches very high concentrations in photoreceptors and cochlear hair cells. Experimental taurine depletion causes retinal degeneration and sensorineural hearing loss, demonstrating its essential role in the development and maintenance of the retina and inner ear. Clinical studies have shown abnormally low plasma taurine levels in individuals with Usher syndrome type II, supporting its specific link to this combined deaf–blind disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Medical genetics",
    "long_answer": "Proteus-like syndrome is a rare congenital mosaic overgrowth disorder (usually due to somatic activating AKT1 mutations). Disorders of this type are diagnosed through dysmorphologic examination and confirmed with specialized molecular testing, tasks that fall within the expertise of clinical/medical geneticists, who also coordinate the multidisciplinary care these patients require. Hence the medical specialty most directly responsible for diagnosing and managing Proteus-like syndrome is medical genetics."
}

{
    "short_answer": "British Medical Journal",
    "long_answer": "In 1986 John A. Tibbles and Michael M. Cohen Jr. published the paper “The Proteus syndrome: the Elephant Man diagnosed” in the British Medical Journal (BMJ). This article was the first to argue, on morphological and clinical grounds, that Joseph Merrick’s deformities matched Proteus syndrome rather than neurofibromatosis, thereby establishing the now-accepted diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dorsal carpal wedge osteotomy",
    "long_answer": "In amyoplasia arthrogryposis, the wrists are often fixed in severe flexion and ulnar deviation. When splinting and therapy cannot restore useful extension, surgeons may resect a dorsal wedge from the carpal bones—-a dorsal carpal wedge osteotomy—-and close the osteotomy, thereby bringing the hand into a more extended, functional position. This procedure is widely described as the operative correction for excessive wrist flexion contractures in these children."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Poor; fatal in childhood",
    "long_answer": "Finnish-type PEHO syndrome is a relentlessly progressive neuro-degenerative disorder. Affected infants show early seizures, profound developmental arrest, cerebral and cerebellar atrophy, and optic atrophy that worsen over time. Nearly all reported patients become severely disabled, and most die in childhood or early adolescence. Consequently, the overall prognosis is very poor, with a high likelihood of death in early life."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cyclosporine",
    "long_answer": "Cyclosporine is a calcineurin-inhibiting immunosuppressant that dampens T-cell activation. Systemic cyclosporine is a well-established treatment for birdshot chorioretinopathy to control ocular inflammation, and it is likewise a mainstay in preventing acute and chronic rejection after organ transplantation (e.g., kidney, liver, heart)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "AJR American Journal of Roentgenology",
    "long_answer": "The American Journal of Roentgenology (AJR) is a leading peer-reviewed radiology journal that frequently publishes case reports and original research detailing the CT and MRI appearance of rare musculoskeletal and soft-tissue disorders. Articles describing the radiologic diagnosis of eosinophilic fasciitis—such as MR imaging findings and CT correlations—have appeared in AJR, making it the journal most likely to feature this topic."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tympanostomy tubes",
    "long_answer": "Individuals with primary ciliary dyskinesia often develop chronic otitis media with effusion, leading to conductive hearing loss. A common surgical intervention is the placement of tympanostomy (ventilation) tubes through the tympanic membrane to ventilate the middle ear and drain fluid, thereby improving hearing."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sternum",
    "long_answer": "Among the characteristic skeletal anomalies of complete trisomy 9 is a markedly shortened sternum; this finding is repeatedly documented in clinical descriptions of affected neonates and is considered a typical dysmorphic feature of the condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Performance appraisal",
    "long_answer": "Ergophobia is an anxiety disorder characterized by an excessive, irrational fear of work and workplace situations. Formal performance appraisals—periodic evaluations in which supervisors rate an employee’s job performance—heighten scrutiny and the prospect of negative judgment. This evaluative pressure can intensify anticipatory anxiety and avoidance behaviors, thereby worsening ergophobic symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dihydrofolate reductase inhibition",
    "long_answer": "6-Pyruvoyltetrahydropterin synthase deficiency severely limits de-novo tetrahydrobiopterin (BH4) synthesis, so patients rely on the salvage pathway in which dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR) reduces BH2 back to BH4. Methotrexate is a potent DHFR inhibitor; giving it blocks this salvage step, precipitously lowering BH4 levels and aggravating hyperphenylalaninemia and neurotransmitter deficiency. Therefore methotrexate must be avoided in these patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "JAM2",
    "long_answer": "The gene JAM2 (junctional adhesion molecule-2) encodes a tight-junction protein that is expressed at the intercellular junctions of endothelial cells, particularly in high endothelial venules. Biallelic pathogenic variants in JAM2 have been identified as a cause of Primary familial brain calcification, linking disruption of endothelial tight-junction integrity to cerebral calcium deposition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SLC20A2",
    "long_answer": "Primary familial brain calcification (PFBC) is most often caused by loss-of-function variants in the SLC20A2 gene, which encodes the phosphate transporter PiT-2. Defective PiT-2 disturbs phosphate homeostasis and leads to calcium-phosphate (hydroxy-apatite) crystal deposits in cerebral vessel walls and basal ganglia. These deposits appear as characteristic electron-dense particles that can be directly visualised in brain tissue sections with an electron microscope, linking the ultrastructural finding to the SLC20A2 mutation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Founder effect",
    "long_answer": "In a small or isolated population the founder effect can raise the frequency of a rare pathogenic allele. If one or a few early ancestors carried the mutation that causes acro-oto-radial syndrome, their disproportionately large genetic contribution means more individuals will inherit the allele, so the syndrome appears more often than in the general population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sympathetic function unchanged",
    "long_answer": "In adrenal cortical insufficiency (e.g., Addison disease) the lack of cortisol mainly reduces epinephrine production in the adrenal medulla, but norepinephrine release from sympathetic nerve endings is unaffected. Thus overall sympathetic nervous system activity and its cardiovascular effects remain essentially normal despite the adrenal insufficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pediatrics",
    "long_answer": "Hymenolepiasis is an intestinal tapeworm infection that occurs most frequently in children; its evaluation and treatment (e.g., diagnosis, prescription of anthelmintic agents such as praziquantel, and preventive counseling) are normally managed by a pediatrician, the physician specialist in Pediatrics."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FLAIR MRI",
    "long_answer": "Fluid-Attenuated Inversion Recovery (FLAIR) is an MRI sequence that nulls the signal from cerebrospinal fluid, reducing its masking effect and thereby enhancing visualization of corticospinal tract hyperintensities and other brain/spinal abnormalities characteristic of amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TBC1D24",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the TBC1D24 gene are the established cause of DOORS (Deafness-Onychodystrophy-Osteodystrophy-Seizures) syndrome. Affected individuals typically develop progressive sensorineural hearing loss together with dystrophic or absent fingernails and toenails. Multiple independent families with biallelic TBC1D24 mutations have confirmed this gene-phenotype relationship, making TBC1D24 the canonical mutation leading to the combination of progressive hearing loss and nail dystrophy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Medical genetics",
    "long_answer": "Microcephalic primordial dwarfism, Montreal type is a congenital, ultrarare growth-restriction syndrome caused by pathogenic variants in specific genes. Because it is inherited, requires molecular diagnostic testing, genetic counseling, and multisystem surveillance, the specialty that ordinarily evaluates, diagnoses, and coordinates care for such single-gene disorders is Medical Genetics (clinical genetics)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GH1",
    "long_answer": "The GH1 gene encodes pituitary growth hormone; loss-of-function mutations cause isolated growth-hormone deficiency. Conversely, increased GH1 activity (or therapeutic GH) leads to soft-tissue overgrowth that can narrow the carpal tunnel and compress the median nerve, so GH1 is also reported in genetic-disease databases as being associated with carpal tunnel syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Diabetes mellitus",
    "long_answer": "Extensive pancreatic injury after an episode of acute pancreatitis can destroy endocrine tissue, producing secondary (pancreatogenic) diabetes mellitus. Diabetes—particularly type 1—is a well-documented clinical risk factor for the development of bulimia nervosa, linking it to both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cockayne syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Cockayne syndrome is an autosomal-recessive DNA-repair disorder caused most often by mutations in ERCC6 (CSB) or ERCC8 (CSA). Patients show a characteristic facial appearance with deep-set eyes, thin beaked nose, large ears and an aged, ‘cachectic’ look; severe growth failure and progressive tissue wasting (cachexia) are core clinical features. Thus, the syndrome with the distinctive facial phenotype also lists cachexia among its principal symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "The Merck Manual",
    "long_answer": "The Merck Manual of Diagnosis and Therapy, first issued in 1899, is a comprehensive medical reference that covers a wide range of conditions. Modern editions include musculoskeletal emergencies such as acute compartment syndrome, outlining pathophysiology, clinical signs (e.g., pain out of proportion, pain with passive stretch), diagnostic considerations (compartment pressure measurement), and definitive management (urgent fasciotomy)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sclera",
    "long_answer": "The optic nerve exits the globe by piercing the posterior part of the sclera (at the lamina cribrosa). This fibrous outer coat of the eye is the only layer that is anatomically perforated to allow the optic nerve fibers, along with the central retinal vessels, to leave the eyeball."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Periventricular region",
    "long_answer": "Gray matter heterotopia most frequently appear as nodules of ectopic cortex lining the walls of the lateral ventricles; this periventricular (sub-ependymal) location is by far the commonest site reported in neuroimaging and neuropathologic series."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Endoscopic biliary drainage (ERCP)",
    "long_answer": "The cornerstone of therapy is prompt decompression of the obstructed bile duct, most commonly achieved by endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography (ERCP) with sphincterotomy and/or stent placement. Relieving the obstruction stops the high-pressure leak and allows the biliary fistula to close."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Founder effect",
    "long_answer": "Xeroderma pigmentosum is an autosomal-recessive disorder; in some isolated or historically small populations a single ancestral mutation was carried by a few founders. Genetic isolation and subsequent population expansion can greatly increase the allele’s frequency, producing a higher disease prevalence. This population-level concentration of an allele transmitted from a limited number of founders is known as the founder effect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Severe red–green dyschromatopsia",
    "long_answer": "Leber's hereditary optic neuropathy damages the papillomacular retinal ganglion cells, producing central vision loss accompanied by marked impairment of color discrimination, classically a profound red-green dyschromatopsia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Parkinson's disease",
    "long_answer": "The pin-up model and neo-burlesque icon Dita Von Teese—widely credited with spearheading the modern revival of American Burlesque—has publicly spoken about her mother’s diagnosis of Parkinson’s disease and has supported Parkinson’s charities. Thus, Parkinson’s disease is the neurodegenerative disorder most closely associated with this well-known burlesque revival figure. Parkinson’s disease is a progressive neurodegenerative condition marked by loss of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra, leading to motor symptoms such as tremor, rigidity and bradykinesia." 
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lee Mack",
    "long_answer": "Lee Mack, the English stand-up comedian and star of the sitcom “Not Going Out,” has publicly stated that he was diagnosed with developmental coordination disorder (dyspraxia). Dyspraxia is a neurodevelopmental condition that impairs fine- and gross-motor planning and coordination; Mack has spoken about difficulties with everyday motor tasks, such as learning to ride a bicycle, that are characteristic of this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "End-tidal CO₂",
    "long_answer": "Capnography measures the partial pressure of exhaled carbon dioxide (end-tidal CO₂). During chest compressions, the ETCO₂ value reflects pulmonary blood flow and thus cardiac output; higher or rising ETCO₂ indicates effective CPR, whereas persistently low values suggest inadequate compressions. A sudden jump in ETCO₂ is an early sign of return of spontaneous circulation. Therefore, continuous monitoring of end-tidal CO₂ with capnography is the accepted way to gauge CPR effectiveness, independent of the patient’s underlying condition (e.g., a panic attack)." 
}

{
    "short_answer": "≈40%",
    "long_answer": "Clinical series and registry data indicate that thymic carcinoma has a poor prognosis: only about four out of ten patients survive to five years. Most large studies (e.g., ITMIG database, SEER analyses) report 5-year overall survival rates clustered between 30 % and 50 %, with the commonly cited figure being roughly 40 %."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Protein 4.2",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary spherocytosis most often results from defects in red-cell membrane–skeleton proteins such as spectrin, ankyrin, band 3, and protein 4.2. Among these, protein 4.2 (encoded by EPB42) contains a P-loop (Walker A motif) and specifically binds ATP; ATP binding is required for its normal interaction with the band 3–ankyrin complex that stabilises the erythrocyte membrane. Loss-of-function mutations in protein 4.2 therefore impair this ATP-dependent interaction and are a recognised cause of hereditary spherocytosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Continuous positive airway pressure",
    "long_answer": "Infants with bronchopulmonary dysplasia are typically managed with continuous positive airway pressure (CPAP). CPAP provides a constant distending pressure throughout the respiratory cycle, helping to keep the premature lung’s alveoli open, improve gas exchange, and reduce the risk of further ventilator-induced lung injury compared with conventional positive-pressure ventilation. This gentle, lung-protective strategy is therefore the most commonly employed form of mechanical ventilation in BPD care."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Catecholaminergic Polymorphic Ventricular Tachycardia (CPVT) is a genetically mediated disorder in which ventricular tachyarrhythmias are precipitated by rises in sympathetic (catecholaminergic) tone. The causal mutations (most commonly in RYR2 or CASQ2) make the cardiac sarcoplasmic reticulum abnormally sensitive to catecholamines; when adrenaline or noradrenaline levels increase, abnormal calcium release occurs and triggers bidirectional or polymorphic ventricular tachycardia. Because amphetamines are potent indirect sympathomimetics—they stimulate release and inhibit re-uptake of norepinephrine and, to a lesser extent, epinephrine—they raise circulating catecholamine levels and increase sympathetic drive. Consequently, they are recognized as potential external triggers of the arrhythmias in CPVT. Clinical guidelines and expert reviews advise patients with CPVT to avoid amphetamine-based stimulants (e.g., those used for ADHD or taken illicitly) because they can provoke or exacerbate the characteristic ventricular tachycardia episodes. Thus, while amphetamines do not cause the underlying genetic disease, they can be involved in the arrhythmias seen in CPVT by acting as precipitating factors."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LMNA",
    "long_answer": "The premature-aging (progeroid) disorder referred to here arises from a mutation in the LMNA gene, which encodes the nuclear-lamina proteins lamin A/C. The classic Hutchinson–Gilford progeria mutation (c.1824C>T; p.Gly608Gly) creates a cryptic splice site, producing the aberrant protein ‘progerin’ that destabilizes the nuclear envelope and accelerates cellular senescence, leading to the characteristic early-aging phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Skin biopsy",
    "long_answer": "Because extramammary Paget's disease can look clinically identical to eczema or nonspecific dermatitis, a skin biopsy with histopathological examination is routinely performed to confirm the presence of Paget cells and thereby distinguish EMPD from inflammatory dermatoses."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Magnetic resonance imaging",
    "long_answer": "Structural magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is routinely employed to examine grey- and white-matter volumes, cortical thickness, and other anatomical features in the brains of people with schizoaffective disorder, enabling researchers to detect and quantify structural abnormalities associated with the illness."
}

{
    "short_answer": "None",
    "long_answer": "According to historical medical records and biographies, no woman who served as First Lady of the United States has ever been reported to have died of diphtheria. While the bacterium Corynebacterium diphtheriae claimed the lives of some presidential children (e.g., Ruth Cleveland in 1904), the documented causes of death for First Ladies include pneumonia, tuberculosis, stroke, heart disease, cancer, and other conditions—but not diphtheria."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "There is no scientific or clinical evidence that the measles vaccine (a live-attenuated preparation intended to prevent measles infection) has any therapeutic effect on interstitial cystitis/painful bladder syndrome. Standard evidence-based management of interstitial cystitis includes lifestyle modification, oral agents such as pentosan polysulfate sodium, antihistamines, tricyclic antidepressants, analgesics, and bladder-directed therapies (e.g., dimethyl sulfoxide instillation, heparin/lidocaine cocktails, hydrodistention, neuromodulation, botulinum toxin). While some immunomodulatory approaches—most notably intravesical bacillus Calmette-Guérin (BCG) originally developed as a tuberculosis vaccine—have been investigated experimentally for interstitial cystitis, measles vaccine has never been shown to be effective or recommended by urological or infectious-disease guidelines. Therefore, using the measles vaccine as part of interstitial cystitis treatment is not supported by established biomedical consensus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Ergocalciferol (vitamin D₂) is used experimentally and clinically to correct vitamin D deficiency and to manage hypocalcemia. In animal studies, vitamin D compounds are more often investigated for their potential to improve insulin secretion or insulin sensitivity in states of deficiency. The recognized toxicities of excessive vitamin D intake in animals include hypercalcemia, hyperphosphatemia, soft-tissue calcification, and renal damage, not disturbances leading to elevated circulating insulin. A sustained rise in insulin (hyperinsulinemia) is not a reported or mechanistically expected effect of ergocalciferol in the veterinary or experimental literature. Therefore, according to established scientific knowledge, ergocalciferol does not cause hyperinsulinemia in animals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FA2H",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the FA2H gene (fatty acid 2-hydroxylase) cause the SPG35 subtype of hereditary spastic paraplegia. FA2H encodes the enzyme that 2-hydroxylates long-chain fatty acids, a critical step in sphingolipid and myelin lipid metabolism, linking this HSP gene directly to fatty acid metabolism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Celiac disease",
    "long_answer": "Celiac disease is an autoimmune‐mediated enteropathy triggered by gluten that damages the proximal small-intestinal mucosa. The resulting villous atrophy markedly reduces absorption of water-soluble vitamins, including thiamine (vitamin B1). Profound thiamine deficiency can precipitate Wernicke encephalopathy, so celiac disease is a recognized autoimmune cause of this neurologic syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Postprandial insulin surge",
    "long_answer": "A carbohydrate-rich meal provokes a rapid rise in insulin, which suppresses lipolysis and fatty-acid oxidation. With fats unavailable, working muscle must rely almost entirely on glycolysis and the purine-nucleotide cycle for ATP. In AMPD1 deficiency the purine-nucleotide cycle is blocked (AMP cannot be converted to IMP and fumarate), so glycolytic ATP production falls sharply. The sudden loss of an alternative energy source after the insulin surge therefore produces abrupt fatigue and inability to sustain continuous exercise."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ondine",
    "long_answer": "The disorder was long referred to as “Ondine’s curse,” a term coined after Jean Giraudoux’s 1938 play ‘Ondine,’ in which the water-nymph Ondine condemns her faithless lover to stop breathing whenever he falls asleep—an allegory that parallels the life-threatening hypoventilation during sleep seen in Central Hypoventilation Syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RET proto-oncogene mutation",
    "long_answer": "The combined syndrome of Hirschsprung’s disease with type-D brachydactyly is produced by a heterozygous loss-of-function point mutation in the RET proto-oncogene (10q11.2). This mutation disrupts RET signalling needed for migration of enteric neural crest cells as well as for normal development of the distal phalanx of the thumb, leading respectively to intestinal aganglionosis (Hirschsprung’s disease) and the characteristic short thumb (brachydactyly type D)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cyclosporine",
    "long_answer": "Cyclosporine, a calcineurin-inhibiting immunosuppressant, is reported as an effective systemic therapy for refractory Sweet syndrome. The same drug is routinely used in transplant medicine to prevent organ rejection by suppressing T-cell activation, linking its dermatologic use with its established role in graft prophylaxis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dysphoria",
    "long_answer": "In progressive supranuclear palsy, patients may develop mood and behavioural disturbances. One recognised symptom is dysphoria, which is clinically defined as a profound state of unease or dissatisfaction. This mood disturbance arises from degeneration of frontal-subcortical circuits involved in emotional regulation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NPC1",
    "long_answer": "Niemann–Pick disease type C is most commonly caused by mutations in the NPC1 gene. Loss-of-function NPC1 variants impair intracellular cholesterol trafficking, producing progressive neurodegeneration that eventually involves the brain-stem respiratory centers; many affected individuals ultimately succumb to central hypoventilation and respiratory arrest."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Jeremy Farrar",
    "long_answer": "Dr. Jeremy Farrar, an infectious-disease physician and former director of the Wellcome Trust, was appointed as the World Health Organization’s Chief Scientist in 2023. In this role he oversees the WHO R&D Blueprint, a program that explicitly plans for emerging pathogens—including the hypothetical ‘Disease X’—and he has long advocated for research and global preparedness against such unknown threats."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Norepinephrine",
    "long_answer": "Catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia (CPVT) is precipitated by sympathetic activation. The sympathetic nerve endings that innervate the heart release the catecholamine neurotransmitter norepinephrine, which binds to β-adrenergic receptors on cardiomyocytes. This β-adrenergic stimulation elevates intracellular cAMP and Ca²⁺, promoting delayed after-depolarizations that trigger the ventricular tachyarrhythmias characteristic of CPVT. Thus, norepinephrine is the neurotransmitter responsible for these arrhythmias."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Endocrinology",
    "long_answer": "Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia is a genetic disorder of the calcium-sensing receptor that produces lifelong hypercalcemia resembling primary hyperparathyroidism. Because it involves parathyroid hormone regulation and systemic calcium metabolism, its evaluation (serum calcium, PTH, urinary calcium excretion, genetic testing) is usually undertaken by an endocrinologist—the specialty that manages disorders of hormones, parathyroid glands, and mineral homeostasis—making endocrinology the specialty most likely to establish the diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Iliopsoas muscle",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary spastic paraplegia primarily causes upper-motor-neuron-type spasticity in the lower limbs, but quantitative strength studies show a disproportionate loss of hip-flexor power. The iliopsoas—the principal hip-flexor—is consistently weaker than other muscle groups, producing the characteristic difficulty with stair climbing and initiating the swing phase of gait that patients report. This marked iliopsoas weakness is therefore a recognized clinical feature of hereditary spastic paraplegia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IDUA",
    "long_answer": "Hurler syndrome (MPS-I H) and Pseudo-Hurler polydystrophy (the milder Scheie form of MPS-I) are allelic disorders.  Both are caused by loss-of-function mutations in the IDUA gene, which encodes the lysosomal enzyme α-L-iduronidase.  Severe IDUA mutations abolish enzyme activity and give the classic Hurler phenotype, whereas milder mutations leave residual enzyme activity and produce the Scheie/Pseudo-Hurler polydystrophy presentation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Immunology",
    "long_answer": "Omenn syndrome is a form of severe combined immunodeficiency caused by mutations that cripple adaptive immunity. Because its management requires expertise in diagnosing and treating primary immune-deficiency diseases (e.g., immunoglobulin replacement, infection prophylaxis, and hematopoietic stem-cell transplantation), care is principally provided by the medical specialty of Immunology (often clinical or pediatric immunology/allergy)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Heart",
    "long_answer": "Cardiomyopathy is a disease of the myocardium; in Nathalie syndrome this manifests as pathological changes in the heart muscle, so the organ affected is the heart."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Medical genetics",
    "long_answer": "Wiedemann–Rautenstrauch syndrome is a rare congenital progeroid disorder caused by pathogenic variants in genes such as POLR3A. Because it is an inherited, multisystem condition diagnosed in infancy, management and counseling are typically overseen by medical geneticists, making Medical genetics the relevant specialty."
}

{
    "short_answer": "American Journal of Medical Genetics",
    "long_answer": "The first description of Alwadei syndrome appeared in the American Journal of Medical Genetics, where Alwadei and colleagues reported the novel autosomal-recessive disorder with its characteristic neurodevelopmental and dysmorphic features. That publication established the syndrome in the medical literature."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GNAS mutation",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the GNAS gene (encoding the Gsα protein) cause Albright hereditary osteodystrophy and related pseudohypoparathyroidism syndromes. A classic skeletal sign of these disorders is brachydactyly with shortening of the fourth and especially the fifth metacarpal and metatarsal bones. Thus, GNAS mutation is directly associated with abnormalities of the fifth metatarsal bone."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Coxsackievirus",
    "long_answer": "Coxsackieviruses (enteroviruses in the Picornaviridae family) have been documented as triggers of Gianotti–Crosti syndrome and are also among the commonest pathogens isolated from cases of aseptic (viral) meningitis, making them a leading cause of that condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sevnica",
    "long_answer": "Sevnica, a town in eastern Slovenia, is best known for Sevnica Castle, whose municipal museum features a permanent exhibition on the great Croatian-Slovenian Peasant Revolt of 1573. The local population has also been the subject of medical-genetic studies because several extended Sevnica families carry a founder mutation that causes post-axial polydactyly (extra fingers or toes). This links the town both to the historic revolt and to the inherited condition polydactyly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ST-segment elevation",
    "long_answer": "In eosinophilic myocarditis, dense eosinophilic infiltration and necrosis of the ventricular wall produce injury currents that most often manifest on the ECG as ST-segment elevation, frequently mimicking an acute myocardial infarction and reported as the commonest abnormality in published case series."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Thr36Met mutation",
    "long_answer": "A missense change at codon 36 of PKHD1, substituting threonine with methionine (p.Thr36Met, T36M), is the best-characterised allele that produces the most severe, very-early (often prenatal/preterm) presentation of autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease. Homozygosity or compound heterozygosity for T36M is repeatedly linked to massive fetal nephromegaly, oligohydramnios and high perinatal mortality, underscoring its association with preterm manifestation of ARPKD."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hematoxylin-eosin stain",
    "long_answer": "Routine hematoxylin-and-eosin (H&E) staining of a biopsy specimen allows pathologists to identify the characteristic triad of granulomatosis with polyangiitis—necrotizing granulomatous inflammation, small- to medium-vessel vasculitis, and tissue necrosis—making H&E the standard histopathological stain used to confirm the diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Yim–Ebbin syndrome has been documented in siblings born to phenotypically normal parents, with no vertical transmission and with reported parental consanguinity. The occurrence in brothers and sisters of either sex and absence in preceding generations is characteristic of autosomal recessive inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Tucker syndrome (also called cataract-microcephaly syndrome) is transmitted in an autosomal recessive manner—two pathogenic alleles, one inherited from each heterozygous carrier parent, are required for the disorder to manifest."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Definitive histologic diagnosis",
    "long_answer": "In soft-tissue sarcoma management the pathologist’s primary role is to examine the biopsy or resection specimen, confirm that the lesion is a sarcoma, determine its precise histologic subtype and grade, and evaluate resection margins and relevant molecular markers. This definitive histologic diagnosis provides the information surgeons, medical oncologists and radiation oncologists need to decide on the extent of surgery, the use of radiotherapy or chemotherapy, and the patient’s prognosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SHC2 gene",
    "long_answer": "Genome-wide copy-number studies in Japanese cohorts with multiple system atrophy revealed a heterozygous deletion encompassing the SHC2 gene (chromosome 19p13.3). Loss of SHC2, which encodes the neuronal adaptor protein N-Shc involved in neurotrophin signalling, was significantly over-represented in patients who showed severe balance and gait disturbances characteristic of MSA, linking the SHC2 deletion to these balance disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Approximately 25%",
    "long_answer": "European epidemiologic studies show that roughly one-quarter of people with intellectual disability have active epilepsy; prevalence surveys across EU member states consistently converge around 22–26 %, so the commonly cited figure is about 25 %."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Short, broad ribs",
    "long_answer": "Pyknoachondrogenesis is a lethal skeletal dysplasia that combines the extreme limb shortening of achondrogenesis with generalized osteosclerosis (“pykno-”). Radiographically, the thorax is very small because the ribs are severely foreshortened and widened; this characteristic involvement of short, broad ribs is a defining feature of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Heterotopic ossification (HO) after total hip arthroplasty is commonly prevented with EITHER peri-operative low-dose external-beam radiation (a single 700–800 cGy fraction given within 24 h before or up to 72 h after surgery) OR a course of a non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug, classically indomethacin 25 mg three times daily for 1–6 weeks. Large randomized trials and meta-analyses show each modality used alone is effective; there is no consistent evidence that combining them provides superior prophylaxis, and routine combination therapy is not recommended in guidelines because it adds cost, complexity and potential side-effects without proven additional benefit. Moreover, most studies of indomethacin use a course far longer than three days; data supporting only a three-day course are limited and have not replaced the standard longer regimens in consensus statements. Therefore, describing ‘post-operative single low-dose radiation with three days of oral indomethacin’ as the ideal regimen is not supported by established biomedical consensus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "α-sarcoglycan (SGCA)",
    "long_answer": "Among the four sarcoglycan genes (α/SGCA, β/SGCB, γ/SGCG, δ/SGCD), mutations in the α-sarcoglycan gene (LGMD 2D) are reported most frequently in genetically outbred populations, making SGCA the commonest sarcoglycanopathy outside founder or consanguineous groups."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Incomplete penetrance",
    "long_answer": "PH3 is caused by biallelic pathogenic variants in the HOGA1 gene, but many individuals who carry these variants never develop symptomatic hyperoxaluria or kidney stones. Compared with PH1 and PH2, the proportion of genetically affected individuals who manifest disease is much lower, so PH3 is classified as having incomplete (low) penetrance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "American Academy of Pediatrics",
    "long_answer": "Because children with hemihyperplasia have an elevated risk of embryonal tumors such as Wilms tumor and hepatoblastoma, the American Academy of Pediatrics (AAP) issued clinical guidelines that include a specific tumor-screening protocol—typically serial abdominal ultrasonography and α-fetoprotein measurement at defined intervals during early childhood—to facilitate early tumor detection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fulminant hepatitis",
    "long_answer": "When hepatitis D virus infects an individual who already carries hepatitis B virus, the combined infection frequently precipitates fulminant hepatitis—an abrupt, massive hepatic necrosis that can progress within days to acute liver failure. Fulminant hepatitis is characteristically accompanied by rapid-onset hepatic encephalopathy (brain swelling/inflammation due to accumulated neurotoxins), making it one of the most feared and lethal complications of HBV–HDV superinfection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Syringomyelia",
    "long_answer": "Chiari malformation frequently leads to the formation of a fluid-filled cavity (syrinx) within the spinal cord, a condition called syringomyelia. Expansion of the syrinx compresses and disrupts spinal cord tracts, including the corticospinal pathways, producing muscle weakness and paresis—i.e., a partial loss of voluntary movement."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Microcornea, early-onset glaucoma and absent frontal sinuses are classic features of Axenfeld–Rieger (Rieger) syndrome. The condition results from heterozygous mutations in genes such as PITX2 or FOXC1, and these mutations are transmitted in an autosomal dominant fashion with variable expressivity. Therefore, the likely mode of inheritance for this disorder is autosomal dominant."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Serous fluid from a chancre",
    "long_answer": "Dark-field microscopy is performed on the clear serous exudate expressed from a primary syphilitic chancre (or other moist lesion). This freshly collected fluid contains live motile Treponema pallidum organisms that can be visualised immediately under dark-field illumination, allowing rapid bedside diagnosis of syphilis before serological tests become positive."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NTRK1 (TrkA)",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the NTRK1 gene, which encodes the high-affinity nerve growth factor receptor TrkA, underlie hereditary sensory and autonomic neuropathy with intellectual disability (HSAN ID/HSAN type IV). TrkA is a receptor tyrosine kinase that mediates NGF signalling required for axon guidance, target innervation and the formation and maintenance of synapses in developing sensory and sympathetic neurons. Loss-of-function of this receptor therefore disrupts axonal outgrowth and synaptogenesis, producing the characteristic sensory and autonomic deficits observed in HSAN ID patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Greek",
    "long_answer": "The clinical term for the involuntary passage of feces in children aged four years or older is “encopresis.” This word derives from the Greek roots en- (meaning “in”) and kopros (meaning “feces” or “dung”), so its origin language is Greek."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Valea Lungă",
    "long_answer": "Valea Lungă is a commune in Alba County situated only a few kilometres from the town of Blaj. It is best known for its 14th-century fortified church, officially listed as a historic monument, which makes the commune notable for medieval heritage in the region."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Reduced aortomesenteric angle",
    "long_answer": "Lumbar hyperlordosis increases the anterior curvature of the spine, pulling the superior mesenteric artery anteriorly while the aorta remains posterior. This spinal change narrows the normal 25–60° aortomesenteric angle and shortens the distance between the vessels. The resulting reduced aortomesenteric angle compresses the third portion of the duodenum between the SMA and the aorta, thereby predisposing an individual to Superior Mesenteric Artery (SMA) syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ten cases",
    "long_answer": "Kapur–Toriello syndrome is an exceptionally rare multiple-congenital-anomaly disorder first reported by Kapur and Toriello in 1991. A survey of the published medical literature shows that, since that original description, only about ten individual patients have been documented worldwide, underscoring the extreme rarity of the condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Primapterin",
    "long_answer": "In Pterin-4-α-carbinolamine dehydratase (PCBD) deficiency the unmetabolised 4-α-carbinolamine rearranges non-enzymatically to the pterin derivative primapterin (also called 7-biopterin). Urinary primapterin levels become markedly elevated, a finding not seen in other tetrahydrobiopterin (BH4) deficiencies; therefore raised primapterin is a specific biochemical marker that distinguishes PCBD deficiency from other BH4 disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Aulus Cornelius Celsus",
    "long_answer": "In his 1st-century medical treatise De Medicina, the Roman physician Aulus Cornelius Celsus classified several kinds of verrucae (warts) and specifically described the deep, painful plantar wart he called “myrmecia.” His account is the earliest known medical description of this wart type."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bone morphogenetic protein (BMP)",
    "long_answer": "During limb development, Bone Morphogenetic Proteins—particularly BMP-2, BMP-4 and BMP-7—activate apoptosis in the interdigital mesenchyme. This programmed cell death removes the webbing between the developing fingers and toes, ensuring normal separation of the digits. Failure of BMP-driven apoptosis leads to syndactyly (fused digits), underscoring BMPs’ central role in normal digit separation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Aneurysm",
    "long_answer": "An aneurysm is a localized outward bulging (dilatation) of a blood vessel wall. When an aneurysm occurs in vessels close to the duodenum—such as the abdominal aorta or the superior mesenteric artery—it can externally compress the third part of the duodenum, producing functional obstruction and marked dilatation proximal to the blockage, a condition termed megaduodenum."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Imperforate hymen",
    "long_answer": "An imperforate hymen is a congenital obstructive anomaly of the vaginal outlet. Because the hymenal membrane lacks an opening, menstrual blood cannot exit, leading to its accumulation in the vagina (hematocolpos). The progressively enlarging, blood-filled vagina and uterus can stretch pelvic nerves and compress adjacent structures, producing lower-abdominal and musculoskeletal pain that patients often describe as muscle cramps. Thus, the same condition explains both hematocolpos and the accompanying muscle cramps."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Growth hormone",
    "long_answer": "Children with Cockayne syndrome often have deficiency of pituitary growth hormone, contributing to their severe post-natal growth failure. Recombinant human growth hormone replacement can restore IGF-1 production and promote linear growth, so GH therapy is considered a potential treatment for the stature deficits seen in these patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Inframammary fold",
    "long_answer": "Before reconstructive surgery for congenital amastia, the surgeon draws the intended inframammary fold on the standing patient. This pre-operative marking serves as a fixed landmark during the operation, guiding pocket dissection and ensuring the reconstructed breast mound is positioned at the correct level on the chest wall."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cefaclor",
    "long_answer": "Numerous case-series and reports have documented that the second-generation cephalosporin antibiotic cefaclor can trigger an IgA-mediated small-vessel vasculitis clinically identical to Henoch–Schönlein purpura (IgA vasculitis). The drug induces immune-complex formation and deposition in post-capillary venules, leading to the characteristic palpable purpura, arthralgia, abdominal pain and renal involvement seen in HSP. Consequently, cefaclor is the antibiotic most frequently cited as being causally linked to the development of Henoch–Schönlein purpura."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Unknown origin",
    "long_answer": "The Oxford English Dictionary lists the Middle-English adjective “badde” (modern “bad”) as first recorded in the early 14th century but explicitly states that its etymology is ‘uncertain’ or ‘unknown’; no definitive Old English or Proto-Germanic root can be established, though several speculative connections (e.g., to OE bǣddel) are mentioned and dismissed as unproven."
}

{
    "short_answer": "BCS1L",
    "long_answer": "Crandall syndrome—characterised by sensorineural deafness, pili torti–related alopecia, and hypogonadism—has been shown to result from biallelic pathogenic variants in the mitochondrial complex-III assembly factor gene BCS1L. Mutations in BCS1L impair assembly of the respiratory-chain complex, producing the clinical triad that defines Crandall syndrome and its allelic counterpart, Bjornstad syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X-ray radiography",
    "long_answer": "Plain X-ray radiography of the hands is routinely used to visualise bone length and morphology; it clearly shows the short, broad distal phalanx characteristic of brachydactyly type D, allowing detailed assessment of the skeletal structure in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chief complaint",
    "long_answer": "In clinical documentation, the very first symptom or issue that prompts a patient to seek medical attention—whether for eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis or any other condition—is called the “chief complaint.” It captures the patient’s primary concern at presentation before detailed history-taking and examination."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Quinism",
    "long_answer": "Quinism is the toxic syndrome produced by excessive exposure to quinoline-based antimalarial drugs—including mefloquine, quinacrine and chloroquine. Manifestations such as tinnitus, visual disturbances, headache, nausea, and neuro-psychiatric effects arise from the drugs’ interference with neuronal ion channels and retinal function. Thus, intoxication with any of these medications leads to the condition known as quinism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Papillary dermis",
    "long_answer": "In lymphangioma circumscriptum, the characteristic vesicles are produced by markedly dilated superficial lymphatic channels situated within the papillary (superficial) dermis; these vessels communicate with deeper subcutaneous cisterns. Thus, the lymphatic vessels involved reside in the papillary dermal layer of the skin."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NLRP3 (CIAS1)",
    "long_answer": "Both Neonatal-onset multisystem inflammatory disease (NOMID/CINCA) and Marshall (cryopyrin-associated) syndrome belong to the cryopyrin-associated periodic syndromes and are produced by gain-of-function mutations in the NLRP3 (also called CIAS1) gene. The mutant cryopyrin protein causes constitutive activation of the NLRP3 inflammasome, excessive IL-1β release, and the systemic inflammatory manifestations seen in both disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Periodic acid–Schiff stain",
    "long_answer": "Glycogen is a polysaccharide that reacts strongly with the Periodic acid–Schiff (PAS) histochemical stain, turning the accumulated deposits a bright magenta color under light microscopy. A liver biopsy section stained with PAS therefore allows direct visualization of the abnormal intracytoplasmic glycogen accumulation characteristic of Glycogen storage disease type III."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tragus",
    "long_answer": "The tragus is the small, pointed cartilaginous eminence located on the anterior margin of the external ear canal. In several craniofacial conditions, including Nager acrofacial dysostosis (Nager syndrome), patients can present with accessory tragi—extra small tragus-like projections—highlighting that the tragus is the auricular structure in question."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "FibroTest (FibroSure) is a serum-biomarker panel that has been validated for staging liver fibrosis in chronic hepatitis C, chronic hepatitis B, alcoholic liver disease, and non-alcoholic fatty liver disease. For chronic hepatitis D, however, there are no large, well-designed validation studies and it is not endorsed by major clinical-practice guidelines (EASL, AASLD) for fibrosis assessment in HDV-infected patients. Current guidelines recommend liver biopsy or transient elastography as the preferred methods, and they caution that serum marker panels developed for HBV or HCV should not be assumed to have the same accuracy in HDV. Therefore, on the basis of established consensus, FibroTest is not considered a reliable or recommended tool for assessing liver fibrosis in patients with chronic hepatitis D."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurology",
    "long_answer": "Benign familial neonatal seizures are an epileptic disorder that presents with seizures in the newborn period. Evaluation (EEG, genetic testing) and pharmacologic management of seizure disorders are the remit of neurologists—particularly pediatric/child neurologists—whose specialty encompasses diagnosis and treatment of diseases of the nervous system. Hence, Neurology is the medical specialty responsible for managing benign familial neonatal seizures."
}

{
    "short_answer": "National Institutes of Health",
    "long_answer": "Investigators at the U.S. National Institutes of Health—particularly within the National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases—have performed much of the foundational work on systemic capillary leak syndrome, identifying endothelial-barrier defects, cytokine surges, and associated monoclonal gammopathy that underlie the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Topiramate",
    "long_answer": "Topiramate, an anticonvulsant that is also approved (in combination with phentermine) for chronic weight management, inhibits renal carbonic anhydrase. This decreases collecting-duct responsiveness to vasopressin, so patients can develop polyuria and hypernatremia characteristic of nephrogenic diabetes insipidus. Multiple case reports have documented topiramate-induced nephrogenic DI, making it a recognized though uncommon adverse effect of this obesity medication."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Parental consanguinity",
    "long_answer": "Van Den Berghe-Dequeker syndrome (a spondylometaphyseal dysplasia) is transmitted as an autosomal-recessive trait. When the parents are consanguineous, they are more likely to carry the same pathogenic allele, markedly increasing the chance that a child will inherit the mutation from both parents and manifest the disorder. Consequently, consanguinity is considered a significant risk factor for developing Van Den Berghe-Dequeker syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fibula",
    "long_answer": "Léri–Weill dyschondrosteosis (SHOX-deficiency mesomelic dwarfism) produces characteristic mesomelic shortening with lateral bowing of the radius in the forearm and of the tibia in the lower leg, but the fibula remains straight (often appearing relatively long). Thus, the fibula is the long bone that is spared from bowing in this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sarcoplasmic reticulum",
    "long_answer": "In arthrogryposis multiplex congenita, biochemical studies of affected skeletal muscle show defective calcium uptake and release by the sarcoplasmic reticulum. This abnormal sarcoplasmic-reticulum function disrupts the normal contraction-relaxation cycle of the muscle fibre and contributes to the fixed joint contractures seen in the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Penn Medicine",
    "long_answer": "Pennsylvania Hospital—the first hospital founded in the United States (1751)—is part of the Penn Medicine (University of Pennsylvania Health System). This health system offers specialized dermatology-oncology services, including phototherapy, topical and systemic therapies, and extracorporeal photopheresis used in the treatment of mycosis fungoides, a cutaneous T-cell lymphoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive inheritance",
    "long_answer": "Dihydropyrimidine dehydrogenase (DPD) deficiency results from biallelic pathogenic variants in the DPYD gene and is passed on in an autosomal recessive fashion. Because each child of carrier parents has a 25 % risk of being affected and relatives may be asymptomatic carriers, affected patients and their families are offered genetic counseling to discuss recurrence risks, carrier testing, and reproductive options."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HNF4A mutation",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the hepatocyte nuclear factor-4 alpha (HNF4A) gene, which cause MODY type 1, impair regulation of several apolipoprotein and lipogenic genes in the liver. This deranged transcriptional control can raise circulating triglyceride and cholesterol levels, so HNF4A variants are well documented to present with both maturity-onset diabetes of the young and hyperlipidemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "recovery position",
    "long_answer": "After reflex syncope, the airway can be blocked by the tongue or vomitus if the person remains supine. Placing the patient in the recovery position—lying on their side with head tilted and chin extended—uses gravity to keep the tongue clear of the posterior pharynx and allows fluid drainage, thereby preventing airway obstruction."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Transverse plane",
    "long_answer": "The transverse (axial or horizontal) anatomical plane slices the body perpendicular to its long axis, separating superior (cranial) from inferior (caudal) portions. In imaging studies for conditions such as Parry–Romberg syndrome, axial MRI or CT scans acquired in this plane allow clear assessment of asymmetrical atrophy and structural changes across upper- versus lower-level facial tissues."
}

{
    "short_answer": "3-Hydroxy-3-methylglutaryl-CoA lyase",
    "long_answer": "In leucine catabolism, 3-methylglutaconyl-CoA is hydrated to form 3-hydroxy-3-methylglutaryl-CoA (HMG-CoA). The final, committed step that releases the ketone body acetoacetate (along with acetyl-CoA) is the cleavage of HMG-CoA. This reaction is catalyzed by the mitochondrial enzyme 3-hydroxy-3-methylglutaryl-CoA lyase, making it the key enzyme responsible for the ultimate conversion of leucine-derived carbon into acetoacetate."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SHC2",
    "long_answer": "Copy-number variation studies in Japanese cohorts have shown that heterozygous deletions encompassing the SHC2 gene (Shc adaptor protein 2) on chromosome 19p13.3 are significantly enriched in patients with multiple system atrophy. This gene deletion reduces SHC2 dosage, and its loss has been proposed as a genetic factor contributing to the development of multiple system atrophy in Japanese patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HUWE1",
    "long_answer": "Abruzzo–Erickson syndrome is an X-linked intellectual-disability disorder that has been traced to pathogenic variants in the HUWE1 gene, which encodes a HECT-domain E3 ubiquitin-protein ligase on Xp11. Loss- or change-of-function mutations in HUWE1 impair protein ubiquitination and neuronal development, producing the characteristic cognitive delay, growth deficit and dysmorphic features observed in Abruzzo–Erickson syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "No significant association",
    "long_answer": "Epidemiologic studies show that patients with exercise-induced anaphylaxis are not more likely to have atopic diseases (asthma, allergic rhinitis, eczema) than the general population; thus, atopy does not predispose to, or protect from, exercise-induced anaphylaxis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "al (albino) gene",
    "long_answer": "In sweet cherry (Prunus avium) the white, non-photosynthetic “albino” seedlings arise when the recessive nuclear albino allele (symbol al) is homozygous. Molecular studies have shown that this locus encodes a defective form of the magnesium-chelatase subunit I (CHLI), an enzyme essential for the first committed step of chlorophyll biosynthesis. Loss of CHLI activity prevents chlorophyll formation, so seedlings carrying al/al develop completely white tissues and die soon after germination. Thus, the al (albino) gene is the genetic determinant responsible for albinism in the sweet cherry tree."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ultraviolet light",
    "long_answer": "Chronic exposure to ultraviolet (UV) light has been cited as an environmental factor that can promote abnormal follicular keratinization and retention of vellus hairs, thereby contributing to the development of trichostasis spinulosa."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Langer–Giedion syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Langer–Giedion syndrome (also called trichorhinophalangeal syndrome type II) results from a micro-deletion on chromosome 8q24 that removes the TRPS1 and EXT1 genes. The syndrome’s clinical picture includes craniofacial anomalies, short stature, multiple exostoses and characteristic hand abnormalities such as brachydactyly and clinodactyly, making it the chromosome 8 deletion disorder classically linked to clinodactyly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Glucose tablets",
    "long_answer": "For a conscious person with hypoglycaemia, swallowing 15–20 g of pure glucose in tablet form is recommended because the monosaccharide requires no enzymatic digestion, dissolves rapidly, is absorbed almost completely across the proximal small intestine, and reaches peak plasma levels within minutes. This yields near-100 % oral bioavailability and corrects blood glucose faster than sucrose, starches, or sugary beverages, making glucose tablets the preferred method endorsed by diabetes treatment guidelines."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Immunogold labeling",
    "long_answer": "In scanning-electron microscopy, HIV is identified by attaching antibodies specific for viral proteins (e.g., gp120 or p24) to colloidal gold particles. These electron-dense gold tags bind to the virions and appear as bright, highly scattering spots under the SEM, allowing the presence of HIV to be visualised and confirmed. This antibody-gold conjugate technique—immunogold labeling—is the standard means of detecting HIV in samples examined with a scanning electron microscope."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurofibromatosis type 1",
    "long_answer": "Neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1) is an autosomal-dominant genetic disorder caused by pathogenic variants in the NF1 gene on chromosome 17. Patients commonly develop cutaneous or plexiform neurofibromas, and a subcutaneous plexiform neurofibroma in the lumbosacral area can present as a soft midline mass and be clinically mistaken for a dermoid cyst. NF1 is also well known for musculoskeletal manifestations, including vertebral dysplasia and progressive scoliosis. Thus NF1 links both findings—vertebral anomalies such as scoliosis and a lumbosacral mass that can mimic a dermoid cyst."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Follicle-stimulating hormone",
    "long_answer": "Small supernumerary marker chromosomes can disrupt normal gonadal development and function, leading to primary testicular or ovarian failure. This gonadal dysfunction is typically accompanied by markedly elevated serum levels of follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH), a pituitary gonadotropin whose rise signals impaired gametogenesis. Therefore, FSH is the hormone most consistently associated with the infertility seen in individuals carrying small supernumerary marker chromosomes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NMDA receptor",
    "long_answer": "During chronic benzodiazepine use, the brain adapts by down-regulating GABA_A inhibition and up-regulating excitatory glutamatergic signaling. When the drug is stopped abruptly, this imbalance results in excessive activation of N-methyl-D-aspartate (NMDA) receptors, producing the characteristic withdrawal symptoms such as anxiety, tremor and seizures. Thus, NMDA receptor overactivation is a key mechanism in benzodiazepine withdrawal."
}
